// Spoken answers (60-90 s) for git_production guide
(window.SPOKEN = window.SPOKEN || {})['git_production'] = {
  1: {
    a: `Git is a content-addressable key-value store with a version control interface on top. Everything lives in the .git directory as objects identified by the SHA hash of their content. There are four object types. A blob stores the contents of a file, with no name or metadata. A tree represents a directory: a list of entries mapping names and modes to blobs or other trees. A commit points to one top-level tree, which is the snapshot of the project, to zero or more parent commits, and holds the author, committer, timestamp, and message. An annotated tag points to another object, typically a commit, with a name and message. Because identical content hashes to the same ID, Git deduplicates automatically, and because each commit's ID depends on its tree and parents, history is tamper-evident: changing anything changes every descendant hash. Objects start as loose files, zlib-compressed, then git gc packs them into packfiles with delta compression. Branches, tags, and HEAD are just small files containing hashes or references under .git/refs. The index, or staging area, is a binary file describing the next commit's tree. This model explains why branching is cheap, why rebase creates new commits rather than moving them, and why git recovers from mistakes via the reflog.`,
    f: ["Why are branches so cheap in Git?","What does a commit object contain?"]
  },
  2: {
    a: `These commands differ in how they bring remote changes in. git fetch downloads new commits, branches, and tags from the remote and updates your remote-tracking references, such as origin/main, but doesn't touch your local branches or working tree. It's safe and lets you inspect what changed with git log or git diff before integrating. git pull is a fetch followed by an integration step into your current branch; by default that integration is a merge, so if both sides have new commits you get a merge commit, which can add noise to history. git pull --rebase fetches and then rebases your local commits on top of the updated remote branch, replaying them one by one, producing a linear history without merge commits. It's what many teams prefer for keeping feature branches tidy, and can be made default with pull.rebase true. Risks: rebase rewrites your local commits, so don't do it for commits that others already based work on; conflicts may need resolving per commit; and a merge-based pull preserves exact history. In production workflows I prefer to fetch explicitly, look at what came in, and then choose merge or rebase deliberately. Also pull.ff only is a safe setting that refuses to create unexpected merge commits. Understanding the difference prevents surprise merge commits and lost work.`,
    f: ["Why might you prefer fetch over pull?","When is pull --rebase risky?"]
  },
  3: {
    a: `Git has three main areas where your files live. The working tree is the actual directory of files you edit on disk. The staging area, also called the index, is a snapshot of what will go into the next commit: you add changes selectively with git add, even individual hunks with git add -p, so each commit can be a logical unit. The repository, or object database, holds committed history; HEAD is a pointer to the current commit, normally via a branch reference, so HEAD points to a branch, which points to a commit. Commands move data between these areas: git add copies from working tree to index; git commit turns the index into a commit and moves the branch forward; git restore --staged unstages; git restore file discards working tree changes; git checkout or switch updates HEAD and working tree; git diff compares working tree to index, git diff --staged compares index to HEAD, and git diff HEAD compares working tree to HEAD. The three reset modes map onto these: soft moves only the branch, mixed also resets the index, and hard resets the working tree too, which can discard uncommitted work.`,
    f: ["What do the three modes of git reset change?","How do you stage only part of a file?"]
  },
  4: {
    a: `Detached HEAD means HEAD points directly at a commit instead of at a branch name. Normally HEAD references a branch, and new commits move that branch. When detached, you can look around and make commits, but no branch tracks them, so once you switch away, those commits become unreachable and are eventually garbage collected, although the reflog remembers them for a while. How you get there: checking out a specific commit hash, git checkout v1.2.0 for a tag, checking out a remote branch like origin/main directly, or during operations such as a rebase, bisect, or submodule update. CI systems often check out a commit this way too. Getting out. If you made no changes worth keeping, just switch back with git switch main. If you made commits you want to keep, create a branch right where you are: git switch -c rescue-branch, or git branch rescue-branch, which attaches them to a name. If you already switched away and lost track, use git reflog to find the commit hash and create a branch from it. Detached HEAD is useful for inspecting old versions, testing a specific tag, or bisecting, as long as you know what you're doing. Git warns when you enter the state, and git status tells you.`,
    f: ["How do you rescue commits made in a detached HEAD?","Why is detached HEAD common in CI?"]
  },
  5: {
    a: `git stash temporarily shelves your uncommitted changes, both staged and unstaged tracked changes, restoring a clean working tree so you can switch tasks, then lets you bring them back. Basic use: git stash push -m 'message' saves the changes; git stash list shows entries; git stash pop applies the latest and removes it, while git stash apply applies it and keeps the entry; git stash drop removes one. Useful flags: -u includes untracked files, -a adds ignored files, -p selects hunks interactively, and git stash push -- path stashes only specific files. You can create a branch from a stash with git stash branch, which is handy if it conflicts with later work. In a team setting, effective use means: stashes are local, not shared, so they're not a collaboration tool or a backup; always give descriptive messages, because stash@{0}-style names are easily forgotten; don't let stashes pile up, since old ones are hard to apply as code moves on; prefer a WIP commit on a branch for anything you want to keep more than a day, since commits are visible, pushable, and safer; and be careful with pop when conflicts occur, as the stash is kept in that case. Use stash for quick context switches like urgent hotfixes, or for pulling with uncommitted changes.`,
    f: ["What is the difference between stash pop and apply?","When is a WIP commit better than a stash?"]
  },
  6: {
    a: `Internally a branch is just a tiny file under .git/refs/heads whose content is the 40-character hash of the commit at its tip; creating a branch writes that one small file, which is why branching is nearly instant and cheap. When you commit while on a branch, Git creates the new commit and updates that file to point to it. HEAD normally holds the name of the current branch. A tag is also a reference, stored under .git/refs/tags, pointing to a commit, but it's meant to be fixed: it marks a specific point in history such as a release and doesn't move when you commit. Lightweight tags are just a ref to a commit; annotated tags are full objects with a tagger, date, message, and optional GPG signature, and are what you should use for releases. Branches move; tags stay. That difference matters operationally: you deploy and reference releases by tag, while branches represent ongoing lines of work. Tags aren't pushed by default; you push them explicitly with git push --tags or by name. Moving or deleting a published tag causes confusion because others may already have the old one, so protect release tags with rules. Remote-tracking branches like origin/main are references that record where the remote branch was at the last fetch.`,
    f: ["Why should releases use annotated tags?","What is a remote-tracking branch?"]
  },
  7: {
    a: `These are three common strategies. GitFlow has long-lived main and develop branches, plus feature, release, and hotfix branches. Features merge into develop; a release branch is cut for stabilisation, then merged to main and tagged, and hotfixes branch from main. It suits products with scheduled, versioned releases and multiple supported versions, but it's heavy: many branches, big merges, delayed integration, and it conflicts with continuous delivery. GitHub Flow is simpler: main is always deployable; you create a short-lived feature branch, open a pull request, get review and CI, merge to main, and deploy. It fits web services that deploy continuously. Trunk-based development has developers integrate into a single trunk, main, at least daily, using very short-lived branches of a day or two, or committing directly, with incomplete work hidden behind feature flags. Releases are cut from trunk, or by tagging, with release branches only if needed. Research on DORA metrics links it to higher delivery performance, because small, frequent merges reduce conflicts and integration risk, but it needs strong automated testing, fast CI, feature flags, and discipline. Choosing: for continuously delivered services, I'd choose trunk-based or GitHub Flow; for packaged software with multiple versions in the field, a release-branch model. Whatever you choose, keep branches short-lived, protect main, and automate checks.`,
    f: ["Why does trunk-based development need feature flags?","When does GitFlow still make sense?"]
  },
  8: {
    a: `Protecting main means making sure only reviewed, tested code can reach production-bound history. Key rules on GitHub, GitLab, or similar. Require pull requests before merging, with no direct pushes, including for admins where possible. Require at least one or two approving reviews, and dismiss stale approvals when new commits are pushed, so approved code isn't silently changed. Require code owner review for sensitive paths via CODEOWNERS. Require status checks to pass, such as build, tests, linting, and security scans, and require branches to be up to date before merging, or use a merge queue to avoid semantic conflicts between concurrent merges. Require conversation resolution. Require signed commits where provenance matters. Require linear history if you want to disallow merge commits. Block force pushes and branch deletion, which protect history. Restrict who can push or merge. Apply rules to administrators, and use rulesets at organisation level so every repository gets the baseline. Also protect release tags. Consider requiring deployments to succeed before merge for some workflows. Balance: too many rules slow teams down, so keep checks fast and reliable, and provide an emergency bypass with audit logging for incidents. Monitor changes to protection settings themselves via audit logs, because disabling them is a common step in attacks. Document the policy.`,
    f: ["Why dismiss stale approvals?","What does a merge queue solve?"]
  },
  9: {
    a: `Long-running feature branches diverge from main over time, and the longer they live, the more problems they create. Merge conflicts grow, and are larger and harder to resolve, often by someone who no longer remembers the context. Integration risk piles up: the first time the feature meets everyone else's changes is at the end, so surprises appear late, such as incompatible refactors or broken assumptions. Review becomes difficult, because a giant pull request with thousands of lines gets superficial review. Testing happens on a stale base, so passing CI on the branch doesn't mean it will pass on main. Work is invisible to the rest of the team, causing duplication and design drift. Releases get delayed and morale suffers on big-bang merges. Mitigations. Prefer short-lived branches and small pull requests merged within a day or two. Use feature flags or branch by abstraction to merge incomplete work to main safely, hidden from users. Slice features into vertical increments that can ship independently. If a long branch is unavoidable, merge or rebase main into it frequently, such as daily, to keep the gap small; consider git rerere to reuse conflict resolutions; set up CI against the integration of the branch and main; and agree on the design early with smaller design reviews.`,
    f: ["How do feature flags avoid long-lived branches?","What does git rerere do?"]
  },
  10: {
    a: `A Git worktree lets one repository have multiple working directories, each with a different branch checked out, sharing the same object database. Create one with git worktree add ../hotfix hotfix-branch, or git worktree add -b new-branch ../path origin/main. Each worktree has its own working files and index and HEAD, but they share history, so there's no duplicate clone and no extra disk for objects, and commits made in one are immediately visible to others. Benefits. Parallel work without stashing: while in the middle of a feature, you can handle an urgent hotfix in a separate directory without disturbing your uncommitted changes. Reviewing a pull request by checking it out in its own directory, running tests while continuing your own work. Running long builds or tests on one branch while editing another. Comparing behaviour across versions side by side. CI or tooling that needs multiple checkouts. Constraints: a branch can only be checked out in one worktree at a time, which prevents conflicting edits; worktrees must be removed with git worktree remove, or pruned with git worktree prune if the directory was deleted manually; submodules have some limitations; and build artefacts and dependencies like node_modules are per directory, so each needs installing. git worktree list shows them all.`,
    f: ["Why can't a branch be checked out in two worktrees?","How do worktrees compare to multiple clones?"]
  },
  11: {
    a: `Both integrate changes from one branch into another, but with different history shapes. Merge creates a new merge commit with two parents, tying the histories together; it preserves the full, true history including when branches diverged, is non-destructive, and doesn't rewrite any existing commits. The downside is a more complex, non-linear history with many merge commits. Rebase takes your branch's commits and replays them on top of another base, creating new commits with new hashes; the result is a clean linear history, as if you'd started from the latest main. It rewrites history, so it's dangerous on shared branches: if others have based work on the old commits, force pushing rebased ones causes duplicates and confusion. The golden rule: never rebase commits that have been pushed and shared, unless the branch is yours alone. When I use each. Rebase my own local or personal feature branch onto main to update it and tidy commits with interactive rebase before opening a pull request. Merge, or squash merge, when integrating the finished feature into main through the pull request, depending on the team's history preference. Merge when updating a shared branch, because it never rewrites. Use fast-forward only merges for linear history.`,
    f: ["What is the golden rule of rebasing?","Why does rebase change commit hashes?"]
  },
  12: {
    a: `Conflicts happen when Git can't automatically combine changes that touch the same lines. My systematic approach. First, understand the situation: git status lists conflicted files, and I check what operation is in progress, merge, rebase, or cherry-pick, and which branches are involved. Before resolving, know the intent of both sides: read git log for each side's commits on those files, and talk to the other author if the logic is unclear. Open each file and look at the markers: the section between the start marker and the separator is my side, the section until the end marker is theirs; a diff3 conflict style, set with merge.conflictStyle diff3 or zdiff3, also shows the common ancestor, which makes the intent much clearer. Resolve deliberately: combine both changes when both are needed, instead of picking one blindly, remove all markers, and then check the result compiles and makes sense. Use a good three-way merge tool, such as VS Code, or git mergetool, for complex cases. Stage the resolved files with git add, and continue with git merge --continue or git rebase --continue. Crucially, run the build and tests after resolving, because textually clean merges can still be semantically broken. If it goes wrong, abort and restart with --abort. Reduce future conflicts with small, frequent merges and rerere.`,
    f: ["What does diff3 style add to conflict markers?","Why must you test after a clean merge?"]
  },
  13: {
    a: `A squash merge takes all the commits from a feature branch and combines them into a single new commit on the target branch, without preserving the individual commits or a merge commit; the branch's history is not linked as ancestry. When it is appropriate. When the branch has noisy, work-in-progress commits like 'fix typo' and 'try again', which add no value to main history, so you want one clean commit per pull request with a well-written message. It keeps main's history linear and readable, with each commit mapping to a pull request, which makes reverting a whole feature a one-commit operation and simplifies release notes and bisecting by feature. It's the default in many teams on GitHub. When a regular merge is better. When the branch contains meaningful, well-structured commits worth preserving for blame and bisect granularity; with long-lived or large branches, where squashing makes one huge commit that hides detail; when several people collaborated on the branch and you want to retain attribution of individual commits, though co-authored-by trailers can help; or when other branches are based on the feature branch, since squashing makes the original commits unrelated to main and can produce confusing conflicts afterwards.`,
    f: ["Why does squashing complicate branches stacked on top?","How do you preserve attribution when squashing?"]
  },
  14: {
    a: `git cherry-pick takes the changes introduced by one or more specific commits and applies them as new commits on the current branch. Internally it computes the diff of the chosen commit relative to its parent and applies that patch, creating a new commit with a different hash but the same message and author, and a new committer. Use it to backport a fix from main to a release branch, to pull a single commit from a feature branch, or to recover a commit from elsewhere. Syntax: git cherry-pick <hash>, ranges with A..B, and -x appends 'cherry picked from commit' to the message, which is useful for traceability in release branches; -n applies without committing; and merge commits need -m to choose a parent. Risks in production. Duplicate commits: the same change exists with two hashes, so when the branches are later merged, Git may handle it fine, but sometimes produces conflicts or confusing history, and git log can't tell they're the same. Missing context: the commit may depend on earlier changes that were not picked, so it applies cleanly but behaves wrongly, or conflicts. Drift between branches: forgetting to cherry-pick a fix leaves branches inconsistent. Hard to audit what's on which branch.`,
    f: ["Why use the -x flag?","What is the risk of cherry-picking dependent commits?"]
  },
  15: {
    a: `Interactive rebase, git rebase -i <base>, opens an editor with a list of commits from the base to HEAD, each with a command, and Git replays them according to your edits. It rewrites history, so use it on commits you haven't shared, or on your own branch. Supported operations: pick, which keeps the commit as is; reword, to change the commit message; edit, to stop at that commit so you can amend its content or split it into several; squash, to combine it with the previous commit and merge the messages; fixup, which is like squash but discards the message; drop, to remove the commit; reordering, by moving lines; exec, to run a command after a commit, for example running tests on every commit to verify each one builds; and in recent versions, label, reset, and merge for rebasing merge structures, plus update-ref for stacked branches. Practical use: before opening a pull request, tidy messy work-in-progress commits into a logical, reviewable sequence; fix up commits using git commit --fixup <hash> and then git rebase -i --autosquash, which automatically arranges and squashes them; and split a large commit with edit and git reset HEAD^. If things go wrong, git rebase --abort restores the original state, and the reflog can recover from mistakes.`,
    f: ["What does autosquash do with fixup commits?","Why use force-with-lease?"]
  },
  16: {
    a: `Good commit messages explain what changed and, most importantly, why, so future readers, including you during an incident, can understand the intent without reading the whole diff. Conventions. A concise subject line of around fifty characters or fewer, in the imperative mood, such as 'Fix race condition in cache invalidation', followed by a blank line and a body wrapped at about seventy-two characters, explaining the motivation, the approach, trade-offs, and side effects, and referencing issues or tickets. The diff shows how; the message explains why. Keep each commit atomic, a single logical change that builds and passes tests, so it can be reverted, cherry-picked, or bisected cleanly. Many teams adopt Conventional Commits, a structured format like feat, fix, docs, refactor, with optional scope and a breaking change marker, which enables automated changelogs and semantic version bumps. Include trailers such as Co-authored-by, Signed-off-by for developer certificate of origin, and issue links. Avoid vague messages like 'update' or 'fixes', and avoid mixing unrelated changes. In production environments, good messages speed up incident investigation with git blame and log, support audits, and make release notes easier. Enforce conventions with a commit-msg hook or CI check such as commitlint, and with pull request templates.`,
    f: ["Why imperative mood in the subject?","What does Conventional Commits enable?"]
  },
  17: {
    a: `git log is the main tool for exploring history, and its options make it far more powerful than the default. Compact views: --oneline for short hashes and subjects, --graph --decorate --all to see the branch structure with labels, and --stat or --patch for file summaries or full diffs. Filtering: by author with --author, date ranges with --since and --until, message text with --grep, file or directory by appending a path after --, and limiting count with -n. Searching the content of changes: -S 'string', the pickaxe, finds commits that added or removed that string, and -G with a regex finds changes matching a pattern, ideal for finding when a function or config value was introduced or removed; -L :funcname:file or -L start,end:file shows the history of a function or line range. Comparing branches: git log main..feature lists commits in feature not in main, and A...B with three dots shows commits unique to either side; --no-merges hides merge commits, --first-parent follows only the mainline, useful for seeing what landed on main; --follow tracks a file across renames. Custom output with --pretty=format. Combine with git show for a single commit, git diff for ranges, and git shortlog for contributor summaries. Define aliases for your favourite views.`,
    f: ["What does the pickaxe option find?","What does --first-parent show?"]
  },
  18: {
    a: `git blame shows, for each line of a file, the commit, author, and date that last modified it. Use it as an entry point to history, not to assign fault. Basic: git blame file; with -L start,end for a range or a function; -w ignores whitespace changes; -M detects lines moved within a file and -C detects lines moved or copied from other files, so the blame points to the original author instead of a refactoring commit. Mass reformatting or renaming commits pollute blame, so a .git-blame-ignore-revs file, configured via blame.ignoreRevsFile, lists those commits to skip. Productive use during incident investigation: identify the line that looks suspicious, blame it to find the commit, then read the commit message, diff, and linked pull request or ticket to understand intent and context, which tells you whether the behaviour was deliberate. Check when it was introduced relative to when the incident started, correlating with deployment times. Then look at related commits in that pull request and whether tests were added. Don't stop at the last change: use git log -L or follow blame to the parent commit with git blame <commit>^ -- file to see earlier history. Use the web UI's blame view with history navigation for convenience.`,
    f: ["What problem does .git-blame-ignore-revs solve?","How does -C improve blame accuracy?"]
  },
  19: {
    a: `git bisect finds the commit that introduced a bug by performing a binary search through history. You tell Git a known bad commit, usually the current one, and a known good commit; Git checks out the midpoint; you test and mark it good or bad; and it halves the range each time. With a thousand commits between good and bad, you need only about ten tests. Manual workflow: git bisect start, git bisect bad, git bisect good <hash>, then repeat the test and mark each step with git bisect good or bad, and when Git reports the first bad commit, run git bisect reset to return to where you were. It can be fully automated: git bisect run <script>, where the script exits with zero for good, one to 124 or 126 and 127 excluded for bad, and 125 to skip an untestable commit; Git runs it at every step, which is ideal with a failing test or a reproduction script. Tips: write a reliable, fast reproduction test first; make sure the build works at each commit, using skip for broken ones; with a messy merge-heavy history, --first-parent can help; and flaky tests mislead the search, so verify the result.`,
    f: ["How many steps does bisecting 1,000 commits take?","What does exit code 125 mean in bisect run?"]
  },
  20: {
    a: `These tools all modify recent history, with different scopes. git commit --amend replaces the most recent commit: it lets you change its message or add forgotten changes by staging them and running amend, producing a new commit with a new hash. Use --no-edit to keep the message. It's safe if the commit isn't pushed or shared; otherwise it requires force pushing. A fixup is a commit created with git commit --fixup <hash> that's meant to correct an earlier commit in the branch, not just the latest; later, git rebase -i --autosquash automatically reorders and squashes it into its target. This is great during code review: you push fixup commits so reviewers see the incremental changes, then squash before merging. git reset moves the current branch pointer to another commit: --soft keeps the changes staged, --mixed, the default, keeps them in the working tree unstaged, and --hard discards them. To undo the last commit but keep the work, use git reset --soft HEAD~1 and recommit differently, or to split a commit apart. Difference: amend and fixup modify content of existing commits, reset moves the branch pointer and is lower-level; reset --hard is destructive for uncommitted work, though the reflog can recover committed states.`,
    f: ["Why is fixup useful during code review?","What is the difference between reset --soft and --mixed?"]
  },
  21: {
    a: `A pull request, or merge request, is a proposal to merge a branch into another, with a space for review, discussion, and automated checks. Typical workflow: create a short-lived branch from main, make commits, push, and open the PR describing the change; CI runs builds, tests, linters, and security scans; reviewers comment, request changes, or approve; the author updates the branch; once approvals and checks pass, it's merged, by merge, squash, or rebase according to policy, and the branch is deleted; deployment may follow automatically. What makes a good PR. Small and focused: one logical change, ideally a few hundred lines at most, because review quality drops sharply with size. A clear title and description: what and why, links to the ticket, how it was tested, screenshots for UI changes, risks, and rollout or rollback notes. Self-reviewed by the author before requesting review, with a green CI build. Well-structured commits that tell a story. Tests included for new behaviour. Backward-compatible and safe to deploy, behind a flag if incomplete. Reviewers should respond promptly, focus on correctness, design, security, and maintainability rather than style, which linters handle, give actionable and kind feedback, and distinguish blocking issues from suggestions. Use templates, CODEOWNERS to route reviews, and draft PRs for early feedback.`,
    f: ["Why does PR size affect review quality?","What belongs in a PR description?"]
  },
  22: {
    a: `Git hooks are scripts that Git runs automatically at certain points in its workflow, stored in .git/hooks. Client-side hooks include pre-commit, which runs before a commit is created and is used for linting, formatting, secret scanning, and quick tests; prepare-commit-msg and commit-msg, which can template or validate messages, for example enforcing Conventional Commits; pre-push, for running tests before pushing; and post-checkout and post-merge, for tasks like installing dependencies. Server-side hooks run on the remote, such as pre-receive and update, which can reject pushes that violate policy, for example force pushes, bad commit messages, or large files, and post-receive, to trigger notifications or deployments. In hosted platforms, server-side hooks are replaced by branch protection rules, CI checks, and webhooks. The challenge in a team: .git/hooks is not versioned or shared, so developers have to install them. Solutions: set core.hooksPath to a directory committed in the repository, or use frameworks like pre-commit, Husky, or Lefthook, which manage hook configuration in a versioned file and install it with one command. Because client hooks can be bypassed with --no-verify and aren't guaranteed to be installed, treat them as developer convenience that gives fast feedback, and enforce the same checks authoritatively in CI and server-side.`,
    f: ["Why can't client-side hooks be relied on for enforcement?","How do you share hooks across a team?"]
  },
  23: {
    a: `A monorepo holds many projects, services, and libraries in a single repository, as at Google and Meta, rather than one repository per project. Benefits: atomic cross-project changes in a single commit, easy code sharing and refactoring, unified tooling and dependency versions, and better visibility. Git-specific challenges as it scales. Size and performance: clone and fetch times grow, and commands like git status and checkout slow down with huge numbers of files and history. Solutions: partial clone with --filter=blob:none, fetching file contents on demand; shallow clones with limited depth; sparse checkout and cone mode, to materialise only the directories you need; the filesystem monitor and untracked cache for faster status; commit-graph and multi-pack index maintenance via git maintenance; and Scalar, which configures many of these. CI: building and testing everything for every change is too slow, so you need affected-target detection with build tools such as Bazel, Nx, Turborepo, or Pants, path filters, and caching, running only what's impacted. Ownership and access: CODEOWNERS for review routing, and permission granularity limited, since access to the repo means access to all code, unless you use tooling. Merge contention: many commits to main cause frequent rebases, so use merge queues. Release and versioning: independent versioning of components requires tooling like changesets. Tags and branch noise.`,
    f: ["What does partial clone do?","How do you avoid building everything on each commit?"]
  },
  24: {
    a: `A submodule embeds another Git repository inside your repository at a specific path, pinned to a particular commit. The superproject records the exact commit hash of the submodule, plus a .gitmodules file with its path and URL. Pitfalls in production. Cloning requires extra steps: git clone --recurse-submodules, or git submodule update --init --recursive afterwards; forgetting this leaves empty directories, a frequent CI failure. The pinned commit is separate from the submodule's branches, so developers commonly end up in a detached HEAD inside the submodule, make commits there, and forget to push the submodule before pushing the superproject, leaving others with a reference to a commit that doesn't exist on the remote. Updating is manual: bumping the pointer needs a commit in the superproject. Merge conflicts on submodule pointers are confusing. Authentication: CI needs credentials to fetch private submodule repositories, and relative URLs can break across forks and mirrors. Tooling and IDE support is inconsistent, and the workflow is hard for newcomers. Mitigation: set submodule.recurse true and push.recurseSubmodules check or on-demand; automate pointer updates with dependency bots; keep submodules few and stable; document the workflow; verify in CI that referenced commits exist. Alternatives: package managers and versioned artefacts for shared libraries, git subtree, which copies code into your repository, or a monorepo.`,
    f: ["Why does a pushed superproject sometimes reference a missing commit?","What alternatives avoid submodules?"]
  },
  25: {
    a: `The reflog is a local log of where HEAD and each branch tip have pointed over time, recording every checkout, commit, reset, rebase, merge, and amend, typically for ninety days for reachable entries and thirty days for unreachable ones. Because Git rarely deletes objects immediately, even commits that no branch references anymore usually still exist until garbage collection, and the reflog is your index to them. It saves you from disasters like: a hard reset that discarded commits; a rebase that went wrong; a deleted branch; an amend that lost content; a detached HEAD session you left. Use: git reflog shows entries like HEAD@{3} with the operation description; find the state before the mistake, then recover with git reset --hard HEAD@{3} to move the branch back, or git branch rescue <hash> or git switch -c rescue <hash> to create a branch at that commit without disturbing the current one, or cherry-pick specific commits. You can also see a branch's own history with git reflog show branchname, and use time-based references such as main@{yesterday}.`,
    f: ["What doesn't the reflog protect?","How long do reflog entries last?"]
  },
  26: {
    a: `On a shared branch like main, the safe way is git revert, not reset. git revert <hash> creates a new commit that applies the inverse of the bad commit, leaving history intact; others who have pulled simply get another commit and nothing breaks. It's the right approach for a production branch where history must not be rewritten. For a merge commit, specify the mainline parent with -m 1, which reverts the changes brought in by the merge relative to the first parent, typically main. Note a subtlety: after reverting a merge, if you later want to re-merge the same branch, Git thinks those changes were already merged and are removed, so you must revert the revert first, or recreate the changes. For several commits, revert each, or a range, in reverse order, possibly with -n to combine them into one commit. Then run tests, push through the normal pull request process, and deploy; in an incident, speed matters, so a revert pull request with an expedited review is the standard. Rewriting history with reset and force push is only acceptable in rare cases, such as removing a committed secret, and then needs coordination, branch protection disabled temporarily, and everyone re-syncing; even then, the secret must be rotated.`,
    f: ["Why does revert beat reset on a shared branch?","What is the catch when reverting a merge commit?"]
  },
  27: {
    a: `Deleting a branch only removes the reference file; the commits remain in the object database until garbage collection, which gives a recovery window. Steps. If you just deleted it, Git often prints the last hash: 'Deleted branch feature (was abc1234)'; recreate with git branch feature abc1234. Otherwise use git reflog to find the last commit the branch pointed to: look for entries mentioning checking out or committing on that branch, such as 'checkout: moving from feature to main', and the hash just before it is the tip. Then git branch feature <hash>. If the reflog doesn't help, for example in another clone, git fsck --lost-found, or git fsck --unreachable, lists dangling commits not reachable from any ref; inspect candidates with git show, and recreate a branch from the right one. If the branch had been pushed, recover from the remote: other clones might have origin/feature remote-tracking refs, and hosting platforms like GitHub keep deleted branch information: GitHub's pull request page has a 'Restore branch' button, and its API and events record the SHA; GitLab has similar options. Ask a teammate to push their copy. Time matters, since git gc prunes unreachable objects after the expiry period, normally two weeks for unreachable loose objects, and thirty days in the reflog.`,
    f: ["How does git fsck help find lost commits?","Where else can you recover a deleted remote branch from?"]
  },
  28: {
    a: `Corruption is rare but can occur from disk failures, interrupted writes, bad filesystems, or damaged transfers. First, diagnose: run git fsck --full, which checks object integrity and connectivity and reports missing or corrupt objects, such as 'missing blob' or 'bad object'. Before changing anything, make a backup copy of the entire directory. Recovery options in order of simplicity. If the remote has a good copy, the easiest is to re-clone, then copy over any unpushed local work: your working tree files, and branches that exist only locally. Alternatively, fetch missing objects: if only a few objects are corrupt, delete the corrupt object file or empty pack, then run git fetch origin, which re-downloads missing objects, and repeat fsck. If a pack file is corrupt, move it away and fetch again. For corrupt refs, check .git/refs and packed-refs, and repair or recreate branch references pointing to valid commits, using the reflog, or git update-ref. A zero-length or damaged index can be recreated with git reset or by deleting .git/index and running git reset. You can reconstruct from another clone of the same repository by copying missing objects across. If objects are truly lost and exist nowhere else, recover file content from working trees or editor history.`,
    f: ["Why back up the directory before repairing?","When is a re-clone the best fix?"]
  },
  29: {
    a: `Git stores every version of every file in history, and binary files like images, videos, datasets, and compiled artefacts don't delta-compress well and can't be merged, so the repository grows quickly, clones and fetches become slow, and operations get heavier; deleting a big file later doesn't shrink history. Git Large File Storage, LFS, addresses this by replacing large files in the repository with small text pointer files, containing the object's hash and size, while the actual content is stored on a separate LFS server or storage and downloaded on demand. You configure which patterns to track with git lfs track '*.psd', which records them in .gitattributes; after installing the git lfs hooks, add and commit as usual. Cloning fetches pointers and then downloads the large files for the checked-out commit only, so history stays light; git lfs fetch --all downloads everything. You need LFS for: design assets, game assets, ML models and datasets, large media, and compiled binaries that must be versioned alongside code.`,
    f: ["What does a pointer file contain?","When would you avoid LFS and use object storage instead?"]
  },
  30: {
    a: `CODEOWNERS is a file in the repository, in the root, .github, or docs directory on GitHub, or the equivalent on GitLab and Bitbucket, that maps file path patterns to the users or teams responsible for them. Syntax is like .gitignore patterns followed by owners, for example 'docs/ @org/docs-team', or '*.tf @org/platform', with later matching rules taking precedence. When a pull request changes files matching a pattern, the platform automatically requests review from the owners, and with branch protection requiring code owner approval, the PR can't merge without their approval. How it scales code review. Routing: the right experts see the right changes without authors needing to know who to ask, which matters in large repositories and monorepos with many teams. Accountability: clear ownership of directories, services, security-sensitive files like CI workflows, authentication code, infrastructure, and the CODEOWNERS file itself. Governance: critical paths get mandatory review by a security or platform team, while the rest follows normal flow. Reduced reviewer fatigue: using team ownership instead of individuals spreads load and avoids single points of failure.`,
    f: ["Why assign teams rather than individuals?","How does CODEOWNERS interact with branch protection?"]
  },
  31: {
    a: `The first priority is to treat the secret as compromised, since once pushed, it may have been copied, scanned, or cached, and removing it from Git doesn't undo exposure. Steps. Revoke or rotate the credential immediately, creating a new one and deploying it before or alongside the cleanup. Check audit logs for the credential's use to see if it was abused, and escalate to an incident if it was. Then clean the repository, for hygiene and to prevent re-exposure. If the commit is local and unpushed, amend or reset. If pushed, remove it from the full history with git filter-repo, the recommended tool, or BFG Repo-Cleaner, using a file or text replacement, then force push all affected branches and tags. Coordinate with the team: everyone must re-clone or carefully rebase, otherwise old history comes back on the next push. Also deal with copies: forks, pull request refs such as refs/pull on GitHub, which may need the hosting provider's support to purge, cached views, CI logs, and clones; GitHub support can remove cached views. Note that even after rewriting, the secret exists in places you don't control, which is why rotation is the real fix.`,
    f: ["Why does rotation matter more than rewriting history?","What tool rewrites history to remove a file?"]
  },
  32: {
    a: `git rebase --onto lets you transplant a range of commits from one base to another, with three arguments: the new base, the old base to exclude, and the branch containing the commits. The form is git rebase --onto <newbase> <oldbase> <branch>. It takes commits that are reachable from branch but not from oldbase, and replays them on top of newbase. Classic scenario: you created feature-b from feature-a, but feature-a was squash-merged into main, so feature-b's history still contains feature-a's old commits, and a plain rebase onto main would try to reapply them and cause conflicts. Fix: git rebase --onto main feature-a feature-b, which says 'take the commits on feature-b that are after feature-a, and put them on main'. Another scenario: moving a topic branch that was started from the wrong branch, such as branched from develop but meant for main; or removing a range of commits from the middle of history: git rebase --onto A B drops commits between A and B when A is an ancestor of B, hence in the form git rebase --onto A B branch.`,
    f: ["When would a plain rebase fail where --onto works?","What do the three arguments mean?"]
  },
  33: {
    a: `Git is the trigger and source of truth for CI/CD. A typical flow. Developers push a feature branch and open a pull request; the hosting platform sends webhooks to the CI system, or runs built-in workflows such as GitHub Actions, which check out the merge result or the branch head, then run lint, unit tests, builds, and security scans; status checks are reported back to the pull request, and branch protection blocks merging until they pass. When merged to main, a push event triggers the main pipeline: build the artefact once, tag it with the commit SHA, run integration tests, publish to a registry, and deploy to staging, then promote to production with approvals or automatically. Tags can trigger release pipelines: pushing v1.4.0 builds the release artefacts, generates a changelog, and publishes. Scheduled triggers run nightly builds or dependency updates. Path filters limit pipelines to changed directories in monorepos. Manual dispatch triggers one-off jobs. In GitOps, CI builds and pushes an image, then updates the image reference in a configuration repository, and a controller like Argo CD detects the Git change and deploys.`,
    f: ["What event triggers a release pipeline?","Why tag artefacts with the commit SHA?"]
  },
  34: {
    a: `Semantic versioning, SemVer, uses version numbers in the form MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH. Increment MAJOR for incompatible, breaking changes to the public API; MINOR for backward-compatible new functionality; and PATCH for backward-compatible bug fixes. Pre-release suffixes like 1.2.0-rc.1 and build metadata are supported. It communicates the impact of upgrading to consumers. Automating releases with Git tags: the tag, such as v1.4.2, marks the commit that is the release. Approaches. Manual tagging triggers a pipeline: pushing an annotated tag starts the release workflow that builds, tests, and publishes artefacts and release notes. Fully automated: adopt Conventional Commits, where feat commits imply a minor bump, fix commits a patch, and a breaking change marker a major bump; tools such as semantic-release, release-please, standard-version, or GitVersion read the commit history since the last tag, calculate the next version, update changelogs and version files, create the tag and a GitHub release, and publish packages, all from CI on merges to main. Release-please opens a release pull request that you merge to cut the release, giving a human gate.`,
    f: ["How do Conventional Commits drive version bumps?","Why must you never move a published tag?"]
  },
  35: {
    a: `In GitOps, Git repositories hold the declarative desired state of infrastructure and applications, and an agent like Argo CD or Flux reconciles clusters to match. Repository structure. A common pattern separates application source repositories from a configuration, or environment, repository. The app repo contains code, Dockerfile, and CI; CI builds an image and then updates the image tag in the config repo. The config repo holds Kubernetes manifests, Helm charts, or Kustomize overlays. A typical layout: a base directory with shared manifests or charts, and overlays or environment directories such as dev, staging, and production, each with patches and values; or a directory per cluster and then per application, plus a bootstrap folder with Argo CD Application or Flux Kustomization definitions, including an app-of-apps or ApplicationSet. Cluster add-ons like ingress and monitoring live in their own area, and tenants' configuration is separated by team or namespace. Promotion between environments happens by pull requests that change the version in the next environment's directory, or by automated image updaters. Branching: many teams use directories per environment on a single main branch, rather than branch-per-environment, which tends to drift.`,
    f: ["Why separate app and config repositories?","Why prefer directories over branches for environments?"]
  },
  36: {
    a: `Runners clone repositories constantly, so their Git configuration affects speed, cost, and security. For performance, use shallow clones with a fetch depth of one, so the job downloads only the commit it needs instead of full history; deepen only for jobs that really need history, such as changelog generation, version calculation from tags, or blame-based tooling. In large repositories, combine partial clone with blob filters and sparse checkout so the runner downloads only the paths required. On persistent runners, keep a cached mirror or reference clone, or place a regional Git mirror close to the runners to cut latency and load on the origin. Skip submodules, tags, and LFS objects unless the job needs them. Always check out the exact commit SHA for determinism. For security, give the job a short-lived, least-privilege token, such as the platform's per-job token or a read-only deploy key, and never a personal access token. Set persist-credentials to false so later steps and uploaded artefacts cannot capture the token from the .git configuration. Treat pull requests from forks as untrusted, and do not give them secrets. Prefer ephemeral runners, so no state or credentials linger between jobs. Pin the checkout action to a commit hash. Avoid uploading the .git directory as an artefact.`,
    f: ["Why set persist-credentials to false?","When do you need full history in CI?"]
  },
  37: {
    a: `GPG commit signing attaches a cryptographic signature to a commit using the author's private key, so others can verify that the commit was made by the holder of that key and hasn't been altered. Without signing, Git's author and committer fields are just text anyone can set, so identity can be forged: git config user.name 'Someone Else' works. Enabling: generate a GPG key, or use SSH signing or Sigstore gitsign, which Git supports now; tell Git which key with user.signingkey, enable signing with commit.gpgsign true and tag.gpgsign true; add the public key to your hosting account so the platform can show a Verified badge. With SSH signing, set gpg.format ssh and use your existing SSH key, which is simpler to adopt. Why it matters. Integrity and provenance: proves the origin of commits and releases, helping defend against impersonation, such as an attacker pushing commits as a maintainer with a stolen token, or a malicious pull request pretending to be from a trusted author. Compliance: some regulations and enterprise policies require signed commits as part of the audit trail. Supply chain security: signed tags for releases let consumers verify authenticity. Enforcement: branch protection rule requiring signed commits on main; and verification in CI or admission policies.`,
    f: ["What does the Verified badge confirm?","What are alternatives to GPG for signing?"]
  },
  38: {
    a: `Auditing Git access and detecting suspicious activity covers both the hosting platform and the repository content. Platform audit logs: GitHub, GitLab, and Bitbucket provide audit logs, with enterprise tiers streaming them to a SIEM; they record logins, token and SSH key creation, permission changes, repository visibility changes, forks, clones, deploy key additions, branch protection changes, secret scanning alerts bypassed, webhook changes, and app installations. Detection ideas: alerts on repository made public, branch protection disabled, new personal access tokens or deploy keys, mass cloning or downloads by one user or token, access from unusual locations or IP addresses, impossible travel, logins without MFA, new OAuth apps with broad scopes, force pushes to protected branches, and unusual activity outside working hours or by dormant accounts. Access hygiene: SSO and mandatory MFA, least privilege through teams rather than individual grants, regular access reviews, removing departed users, short-lived or fine-grained tokens with expiration, and restricting IP ranges. Repository content: secret scanning, push protection, dependency review, and checking for suspicious changes such as modifications to CI workflows, new outbound network calls in build scripts, or obfuscated code, using CODEOWNERS on sensitive paths. Signed commits help attribute changes. Pull audit logs for forensic timelines during incidents. Also monitor third-party integrations and the CI systems that have repository access.`,
    f: ["Which audit events indicate possible exfiltration?","Why review OAuth app installations?"]
  },
  39: {
    a: `Preventing sensitive data from entering a repository requires layers, because any single control can be bypassed. Before commit: educate developers; configure .gitignore for env files, key files, and local configuration, and provide template files like .env.example; use pre-commit hooks running secret scanners such as gitleaks, detect-secrets, or TruffleHog, managed via the pre-commit framework so they are versioned and consistent. At push: platform push protection, such as GitHub secret scanning push protection, which blocks pushes containing recognised secrets and requires explicit bypass with justification; server-side pre-receive hooks on self-hosted servers. In pull requests and CI: required status checks running secret scanners across the diff and the history, plus scanning for PII patterns, large files, and keys; fail the build on findings. Continuous: scheduled full-history scans of all repositories and organisation-wide secret scanning that alerts on existing and new leaks, with validation of whether detected credentials are active. Design to avoid secrets: use secret managers, workload identity, and short-lived credentials, so there is less to leak; provide libraries and templates that fetch secrets at runtime. Also guard sensitive data beyond credentials: customer data in test fixtures, database dumps, and logs; use synthetic or anonymised data. Handle large files with LFS or exclusion rules.`,
    f: ["Why combine local hooks with server-side protection?","How do you reduce secrets that could be committed in the first place?"]
  },
  40: {
    a: `git gc, garbage collection, optimises the repository's storage and removes unneeded objects. What it does. It packs loose objects, the individual zlib-compressed files created by each commit, into packfiles, which store objects efficiently with delta compression between similar versions, drastically reducing disk usage and speeding up operations. It consolidates multiple packs, packs references into a packed-refs file, prunes unreachable objects, meaning those not referenced by any branch, tag, reflog entry, or the stash, once they're older than the grace period, gc.pruneExpire, by default two weeks, and expires old reflog entries, ninety days for reachable and thirty for unreachable ones by default. Git runs git gc --auto automatically after certain operations when thresholds are exceeded, such as many loose objects or packs. It matters in several situations: repositories with large histories, where unoptimised object storage makes clones and fetches slower; servers hosting many repositories, where git maintenance schedules regular background tasks like commit-graph updates, prefetching, and incremental repacking; after rewriting history to remove sensitive data or large files, where you need to expire the reflog and run gc --prune=now --aggressive to actually reclaim space locally, remembering that remotes need their own gc; and when disk space is tight.`,
    f: ["What does git gc prune and when?","Why avoid gc --prune=now casually?"]
  },
  41: {
    a: `A shallow clone downloads only a limited portion of history, for example git clone --depth 1 fetches just the latest commit and its tree, with no ancestors. It saves bandwidth, time, and disk, so it's the default choice for CI, which usually only needs to build the current commit, and for quickly grabbing a large repository to read or build. You can also use --depth N for several commits, --shallow-since a date, or --single-branch to fetch just one branch. Safe use. Know what breaks: commands needing history fail or misbehave, such as git log beyond the depth, git blame, git describe without tags, merge-base calculations, and tools that compute versions from tags, or diff against a base branch, like affected-test detection. If you need those, deepen with git fetch --deepen=N, git fetch --unshallow, or fetch the needed branch and tags explicitly. Pushing from a shallow clone is possible, though history limitations can cause problems with some servers and operations. Alternatives that keep full commit history but defer file contents: a partial clone with --filter=blob:none, which fetches blobs on demand and supports blame and log, generally better for development; or --filter=tree:0 for even less. Use shallow for automation, partial for humans working on big repositories.`,
    f: ["What breaks in a shallow clone?","How does a partial clone differ from a shallow one?"]
  },
  42: {
    a: `Sparse checkout lets you populate the working directory with only a subset of the repository's files, while the full repository metadata still exists. Combined with a partial clone, you also avoid downloading the unneeded file contents. It's valuable in monorepos where a developer or CI job works on a few services among hundreds of directories. Usage: git clone --filter=blob:none --sparse <url>, then git sparse-checkout set services/payments libs/common, which materialises just those directories, plus files at the root; later add more with git sparse-checkout add. Cone mode, the default in modern Git, takes directory names rather than patterns, and is much faster than the pattern-based non-cone mode; it always includes the files in the root and in parent directories of the selected ones. To restore everything, git sparse-checkout disable. Benefits: faster checkout, git status, and editor indexing; smaller disk usage; and less noise. In CI, check out only the paths that the job needs, perhaps determined from changed files. Cautions: build systems that expect dependencies in other directories will fail unless those directories are included, so define sets of directories for each project, ideally through tooling or scripts maintained in the repository; some commands such as git grep only see checked-out files unless told otherwise; and IDEs may be confused.`,
    f: ["What is cone mode?","How would CI decide which paths to include?"]
  },
  43: {
    a: `Windows uses CRLF for line endings, while Linux and macOS use LF. If developers on different systems commit files with different endings, you get noisy diffs where every line appears changed, spurious conflicts, and scripts failing, for example a shell script with CRLF fails with a bad interpreter error. Git offers settings to normalise this. core.autocrlf true, typical on Windows, converts CRLF to LF when committing and LF to CRLF on checkout, so the repository stores LF but the working tree has CRLF. core.autocrlf input, typical on macOS and Linux, converts CRLF to LF on commit but doesn't alter checkout. False does no conversion. Because autocrlf is a per-user setting, a repository-level policy is more reliable: a .gitattributes file committed to the repository, such as '* text=auto' to let Git detect text files and normalise to LF in the repository, with '*.sh text eol=lf' to force LF for scripts that must run on Linux, '*.bat text eol=crlf' for Windows batch files, and '*.png binary' to mark binary files so they're never converted. This ensures all contributors behave consistently regardless of personal settings. For an existing repository with mixed endings, add .gitattributes and run git add --renormalize . to normalise in one commit, and add that commit to the blame ignore list.`,
    f: ["Why is .gitattributes better than autocrlf?","How do you normalise an existing repository?"]
  },
  44: {
    a: `git bundle packages Git objects and references into a single file that can be transferred by any medium and used like a remote repository. It's the way to move repositories where there's no network connection between the machines, such as air-gapped or highly restricted environments, or where only file transfer is allowed, like removable media. Creating: git bundle create repo.bundle --all packages all branches and tags, giving a full backup or transfer; for incremental updates, git bundle create update.bundle main ^<last-synced-commit> or using a range like lastsync..main includes only new commits since the last transfer. Verify the bundle with git bundle verify, which checks it's valid and that the repository has the prerequisite commits. On the destination: git clone repo.bundle my-repo creates a repository from it; to update an existing repository, git fetch update.bundle or git pull update.bundle main, and you can add the bundle as a remote path. Use cases: seeding an air-gapped network with an open-source dependency or internal code, syncing between a secure and a connected environment through a controlled transfer process with scanning, offline backups of repositories, and handing a repository to someone with poor connectivity.`,
    f: ["How do you create an incremental bundle?","What does bundle verify check?"]
  },
  45: {
    a: `Aliases are shortcuts for Git commands, defined in your configuration with git config --global alias.name 'command' or in the [alias] section of .gitconfig. They save typing, enforce good habits, and package complex commands. Useful examples: short forms like st for status, co or sw for switch, br for branch, and ci for commit. A readable log graph: lg = log --graph --oneline --decorate --all. unstage = restore --staged, to remove files from the index. last = log -1 HEAD --stat, to show the most recent commit. amend = commit --amend --no-edit. fixup = commit --fixup, paired with rebase -i --autosquash. pushf = push --force-with-lease, a safer force push. A tidy command for deleting merged branches using a shell alias prefixed with an exclamation mark. who = shortlog -sn for contributor counts. Aliases beginning with ! can run shell commands, allowing powerful multi-step helpers, like syncing a fork or pruning. Beyond aliases, useful settings: pull.rebase, fetch.prune true to remove stale remote-tracking branches, rerere.enabled, merge.conflictStyle zdiff3, push.autoSetupRemote, and init.defaultBranch. In teams, share recommended aliases via documentation or a dotfiles repository rather than a repository-level config, and don't rely on them in scripts, since they differ per machine. Keep them memorable and don't hide dangerous operations behind innocuous names.`,
    f: ["Why prefer force-with-lease in an alias?","How do shell aliases differ from simple ones?"]
  },
  46: {
    a: `A hotfix is an urgent fix for a production problem that can't wait for the normal release train, and the complication is main may contain unreleased work. Workflow. Identify exactly what is running in production, by tag or commit, since that is the base. Create a hotfix branch from the production tag or release branch, not from main if main has moved ahead with unreleased features: git switch -c hotfix/payment-timeout v2.3.1. Make the smallest possible fix, with a test that reproduces the bug; keep the change focused to limit risk. Open a pull request against the release or production branch, get an expedited review, and let CI run. Build the artefact, tag it v2.3.2, deploy through the pipeline, using the same process as normal releases and verifying with monitoring, with a rollback ready. Then, crucially, forward-port the fix to main so it isn't reintroduced by the next release: cherry-pick the fix commit, or merge the hotfix branch back into main, resolving conflicts, and any other active release branches. Record it in the changelog. In trunk-based development with continuous deployment, the hotfix is simply a fast change to main, or a revert of the bad commit, deployed immediately, so the separation disappears.`,
    f: ["Why branch from the production tag rather than main?","What must you do after deploying the hotfix?"]
  },
  47: {
    a: `git format-patch and git am support workflows where changes are exchanged as patch files, famously used by the Linux kernel through mailing lists. git format-patch turns commits into mbox-style patch files, one per commit, each containing the author, date, commit message, and diff. For example, git format-patch -3 produces patches for the last three commits, or git format-patch origin/main creates patches for all commits not in main; -o chooses the output directory, and --cover-letter generates an introductory message for a series. You can send them with git send-email, which mails them to the maintainers or list. The recipient applies them with git am, which reads the mailbox and creates commits preserving the original author, date, and message: git am *.patch or git am < mailbox. If a patch doesn't apply cleanly, git am stops; resolve conflicts, then git am --continue, or --skip, or --abort. The -3 option uses a three-way merge to apply more robustly. Contrast with git apply, which applies a diff to the working tree without creating commits, and git diff output without metadata. Where this is used: projects that review through mailing lists, such as the kernel and Git itself; sharing changes between disconnected systems or repositories without a shared remote; backporting patch series; and archiving.`,
    f: ["What is the difference between git apply and git am?","What does git am do on a failing patch?"]
  },
  48: {
    a: `git bisect run automates the binary search for a regression by running a script at every step, instead of you manually testing each commit. You start with git bisect start, mark the known bad commit, usually HEAD, and a known good one, then git bisect run ./test.sh. The script's exit code tells Git the verdict: zero means good, any code between 1 and 127 except 125 means bad, and 125 means skip this commit because it can't be tested, for example it doesn't build. Git checks out each midpoint, runs the script, and continues until it prints the first bad commit; finish with git bisect reset. Writing a good script: it must be deterministic and fast, build the project if needed, and run the specific failing test or reproduction, not the whole suite. Make it return 125 when the build fails so unrelated broken commits don't mislead. Automating in CI: when a nightly or integration test fails, a job can bisect between the last passing and first failing commit, automatically identify the culprit commit and author, and open an issue or notify them. Many large organisations run automated culprit finding.`,
    f: ["What exit codes does a bisect script use?","Why can flaky tests mislead automated bisecting?"]
  },
  49: {
    a: `If a bad merge is already pushed to a shared branch, don't rewrite history. Use git revert -m 1 <merge-commit>, which creates a new commit that undoes the changes the merge brought in, relative to the first parent, the mainline. Push it through a fast-tracked pull request, and the branch is back to a good state without disturbing anyone who has pulled. Important subtlety: Git records that the feature branch's commits are already merged, so after reverting the merge, merging the same branch again later will not re-apply those changes, because Git treats them as already integrated and your revert as a deliberate later change. To re-introduce the feature, first revert the revert commit, which restores the changes, and then merge any new commits from the branch; or create a new branch with the commits cherry-picked. If the problem is that the merge was done wrongly, for instance with a bad conflict resolution, the alternative is a fixing commit on top that corrects the files, which is simpler than reverting. Rewriting history with reset and force push is a last resort, justified only if sensitive data was merged, and it requires protection to be lifted, communication to all contributors, and everyone to reset their local branches.`,
    f: ["Why can't you simply re-merge a reverted merge?","When is rewriting history justified?"]
  },
  50: {
    a: `Onboarding a repository to production standards is a checklist, and I'd automate it with a template or a configuration-as-code tool. Governance: define the owner team, set CODEOWNERS, and register the repository in the service catalogue. Branch protection or rulesets on main: required pull requests, minimum approvals, code owner review, dismiss stale approvals, required status checks, up-to-date or merge queue, block force pushes and deletions, and signed commits where needed. Access: least privilege through teams, SSO and MFA, no individual grants, and restricted admin rights. Hygiene files: README, LICENSE, CONTRIBUTING, a PR template, issue templates, .gitignore, .gitattributes for line endings and binary files, and .editorconfig. Quality gates: CI pipeline running build, tests, linting, and security scanning including secret scanning with push protection, dependency scanning, and SAST; pre-commit hooks managed via a framework. Commit conventions with commitlint and a defined merge strategy such as squash. Dependency automation using Dependabot or Renovate. Release process: semantic versioning, annotated tags, and automated changelog and releases. Deployment: pipeline and environment protection rules, with secrets stored per environment and OIDC rather than static keys. Observability of the repository: audit log streaming and alerts. Documentation of the workflow and runbooks. Finally, verify with a checklist or a compliance script that reports drift from the standard, and review periodically.`,
    f: ["Which settings would you automate through a template?","How do you detect repositories that drift from the standard?"]
  },
  51: {
    a: `A force push overwrites the remote branch pointer, but the old commits still exist in several places, so recovery is usually possible. Act quickly and calmly. First, stop further pushes to the branch, and tell the team. Sources to recover from. The colleague's own clone: they likely still have the commits locally, and their reflog or their branch has the previous state; they can simply push it back or push to a new branch. Anyone's remote-tracking branch from before the overwrite: git log origin/branch in a clone that hasn't fetched since. The reflog of your own clone if you had pulled previously: git reflog show origin/branch lists previous positions. The hosting platform: GitHub's push events in the audit log and the API show the before SHA for force pushes, and its UI may allow restoring; GitLab shows push events and has a protected-branch history; support can also help. CI logs often print the commit SHA that was built, and pull request pages retain the old commits in their timelines. Once you have the lost SHA, verify it with git show, then restore: git branch recovered <sha> and push that, or reset the shared branch back to it, then integrate the overwriting commits properly by merging or cherry-picking.`,
    f: ["How does force-with-lease protect others' work?","Where can the lost SHA usually be found?"]
  },
  52: {
    a: `The correct remediation puts revoking first and history cleanup second. Step one: assume it's compromised immediately, because automated scanners harvest public pushes within minutes and even private repositories can be cloned by others. Revoke or rotate the key right away, issuing a new one, and update the systems that use it. Step two: check the provider's logs for any use of the old key since it was exposed, and if there's any suspicious activity, escalate to a full incident, looking for new resources, data access, and persistence. Step three: remove it from the repository, which is hygiene rather than protection. Remove it from the current files, replace it with a reference to a secret manager or environment injection. To purge history, use git filter-repo or BFG to rewrite all commits containing it, force push every affected branch and tag, and ask the team to re-clone or reset. Clean up other copies: forks, pull request refs, which on GitHub may need a support request to purge, CI logs and artefacts, caches. Step four: prevent recurrence with pre-commit secret scanning, platform push protection, .gitignore for configuration files, and by preferring workload identity or short-lived credentials so there's no long-lived key to leak. Step five: document and learn from it in a blameless review.`,
    f: ["Why is rotation the real remediation?","What other places can the secret linger after history is rewritten?"]
  },
  53: {
    a: `If I don't remember the SHA of a deleted branch's tip, I search for it using Git's records. First try the reflog: git reflog lists where HEAD has been, and I look for the last entries when I was on that branch, such as commits with recognisable messages or a checkout moving away from the branch name; I can also filter with git reflog | grep branchname. Once I spot the right entry, I verify with git show <sha> or git log <sha>, then recreate the branch with git branch recovered <sha>. If the branch was never checked out in this clone, or the reflog has expired, I look for unreachable commits with git fsck --lost-found, or git fsck --unreachable | grep commit, which lists dangling commits; then I inspect each candidate with git show and look at dates, authors, and messages to identify the tip, preferring the dangling commit that has no children, since that's the end of the chain. Other places: if the branch was ever pushed, check remote-tracking refs in clones, and the hosting platform: GitHub's pull request page keeps the branch commits and offers a restore branch button for merged or closed pull requests, and the repository's activity view shows deleted branches with the SHA.`,
    f: ["What does a dangling commit with no children represent?","Where does the hosting platform keep deleted branch info?"]
  },
  54: {
    a: `When a bad commit is buried twenty commits back and already deployed, I use git revert, which creates a new commit that reverses that one commit's changes, without touching the commits after it. Find the culprit with git log, git bisect, or blame, then git revert <hash>. The revert may conflict if later commits touched the same lines; resolve conflicts carefully, thinking about what the later code now depends on. Test: run the test suite and confirm the specific bad behaviour is fixed and nothing else broke. Open a pull request, get an expedited review, merge, and deploy through the normal pipeline, monitoring metrics and logs. If the situation is urgent and the revert is tricky, consider alternatives in production terms: roll back the deployment to the previous artefact or tag immediately, to restore service, then fix forward calmly; or disable the feature using a flag. Do not use reset and force push on main: it would erase the other nineteen good commits' ordering, or force everyone to resynchronise, and break the audit trail. If the bad commit also included a database migration or data change, reverting the code may not undo the data effects, so plan a forward migration or data repair.`,
    f: ["Why not reset and force push on main?","What if the bad commit included a migration?"]
  },
  55: {
    a: `Bisect finds the commit that introduced a regression by binary search. Setup: I need a good commit where the behaviour was correct, such as the last release tag, and a bad commit, usually HEAD, plus a reliable way to test. Steps: git bisect start; git bisect bad, marking the current commit as bad; git bisect good v2.1.0, marking the older good one. Git checks out the commit halfway between, and tells me roughly how many steps remain. I build and test at this commit, then tell Git git bisect good or git bisect bad; if the commit can't be tested, I use git bisect skip. Git narrows the range and repeats; after log2 of N steps, around ten for a thousand commits, it prints the first bad commit. Then I examine it with git show, look at the diff, message, author, and linked pull request to understand the cause. Finally, git bisect reset returns me to my original branch and state. To automate, git bisect run with a script that exits zero for good and non-zero for bad, returning 125 for untestable commits.`,
    f: ["What if some commits don't build during bisect?","How many steps does bisect need for 1,000 commits?"]
  },
  56: {
    a: `git rebase --onto moves a range of commits to a different base. Its form is git rebase --onto <newbase> <oldbase> <branch>: the commits that are in the branch but not in oldbase are replayed on top of newbase. In production the most common use is cleaning up stacked or mis-based branches. Scenario one: feature-b was started from feature-a, and feature-a was then squash-merged into main. Feature-b still contains feature-a's original commits, which no longer match anything in main, so a normal rebase would replay them and conflict. Run git rebase --onto main feature-a feature-b, meaning take what's on feature-b after feature-a and put it on main, so only feature-b's own commits are moved. Scenario two: I branched from the wrong branch, such as develop instead of main, and need to move my commits to the correct base without dragging along develop's changes. Scenario three: surgically removing a range of commits from a branch's history. Scenario four: moving a hotfix commit series from one release line to another. Precautions: it rewrites history, so use it on your own unshared branches or coordinate; draw the commit graph and confirm the three arguments; make a backup branch first, or rely on the reflog; verify with git log --graph; run tests; and push with --force-with-lease.`,
    f: ["Why does a normal rebase fail after a squash merge of the parent branch?","What three arguments does --onto take?"]
  },
  57: {
    a: `Splitting a large commit into logical pieces makes review, revert, and bisect easier. If the commit is the latest, reset it softly: git reset HEAD~1 (mixed) un-commits it but keeps all the changes in the working tree, unstaged. Then stage and commit in pieces: git add -p to select hunks interactively, answering y, n, or s to split a hunk further, or e to edit it manually; commit with a clear message; repeat for the next logical group; and verify each intermediate commit builds and passes tests, perhaps using git stash --keep-index to test the staged portion alone. If the commit is further back in history, use interactive rebase: git rebase -i <commit>^, mark the target commit as edit, and when the rebase stops there, run git reset HEAD^ to undo the commit while keeping the changes, then create the several smaller commits as above, and finally git rebase --continue to replay the remainder. For splitting by file, git add path then commit works. Tips: separate refactoring, formatting, and behavioural changes into distinct commits; put tests with the code they test; and order the commits so the history reads as a story. After rewriting, if the branch was already pushed, force push with --force-with-lease, and tell reviewers.`,
    f: ["How does git add -p help split a commit?","How do you split a commit that isn't the latest?"]
  },
  58: {
    a: `Cherry-pick applies the changes from a chosen commit onto your current branch as a new commit, with the same message and author but a new hash. It computes the commit's diff against its parent and applies it. The right tool when you need that specific change without bringing along the rest of its branch. A real scenario: a critical security fix lands on main, but production runs release/2.3, which is two minor versions behind, and you can't take everything from main. You switch to release/2.3, run git cherry-pick -x <fix-commit>, resolve any conflicts caused by code drift, test, and ship a patch release. The -x flag records the original commit hash in the message for traceability. Another scenario: someone committed a fix to the wrong branch, so you cherry-pick it to the right one and remove it from the wrong one. Or you want one useful commit from an abandoned feature branch. Risks: it creates duplicate commits with different hashes, so history doesn't show they're the same, and later merging the branches can produce confusing conflicts; the commit might depend on earlier changes that weren't picked, applying cleanly but failing at runtime, so always test; and overuse leads to branches diverging in ways that are hard to audit.`,
    f: ["Why is the -x flag recommended on release branches?","What can go wrong with dependent commits?"]
  },
  59: {
    a: `It depends on whether the lost work was ever staged or committed. Git hard reset discards changes in the working tree and index, so untracked and never-staged modifications are generally gone, since Git never stored them. However, anything that was committed is recoverable through the reflog: git reflog shows previous HEAD positions, and git reset --hard HEAD@{1} or a specific hash restores the previous state. If you had staged files with git add before the reset, Git wrote those file contents to the object database as blobs, even though there's no commit referencing them; they are dangling objects. You can find them with git fsck --lost-found, which writes the dangling blobs into .git/lost-found/other, where you can inspect their content and copy back the files you need, though without filenames, so you'll have to identify them by content. This works until garbage collection prunes them. For truly unstaged edits, look outside Git: your editor or IDE's local history, such as VS Code's timeline or JetBrains Local History, the operating system's backups and trash, snapshots from tools like Time Machine, and file system snapshots. Editors with autosave may have temporary or swap files too.`,
    f: ["What are dangling blobs and how do you find them?","Which uncommitted changes are unrecoverable by Git alone?"]
  },
  60: {
    a: `git reset moves the current branch pointer to a specified commit, and the mode controls what happens to the index and working tree. --soft moves only the branch pointer: the index and working tree stay as they were, so the changes from the undone commits appear as staged and ready to commit again. Useful to combine several commits into one or to redo a commit message, such as git reset --soft HEAD~3 then commit once. --mixed, the default, moves the branch pointer and resets the index to match, but leaves the working tree alone, so the changes remain in your files but unstaged. Useful for un-committing and re-staging selectively, such as splitting a commit. --hard moves the pointer and resets both the index and the working tree to the target commit, so all uncommitted changes to tracked files are lost. Useful to throw away local work and match a known state, such as git reset --hard origin/main after fetching, but dangerous because uncommitted work can't be recovered by Git. Related: --keep resets but refuses if it would overwrite local modifications, a safer hard reset; git restore and git checkout -- file affect only files, not the branch pointer.`,
    f: ["Which reset mode is destructive for uncommitted work?","What does reset --keep do?"]
  },
  61: {
    a: `When a merge, rebase, cherry-pick, or similar operation goes wrong midway, Git records the in-progress state, and you can leave it cleanly. First, check git status, which tells you which operation is in progress and how to continue or abort. To abort and go back to exactly the state before starting: git merge --abort, git rebase --abort, git cherry-pick --abort, git revert --abort, or git am --abort. These discard any partial resolution work, so if you've invested effort resolving conflicts and then realise something is wrong, consider whether you can instead fix forward. For a rebase, you can also use --skip to drop the currently conflicting commit, which is appropriate if its changes are already upstream. If you've already completed the rebase or merge and find it's wrong, recover with the reflog: git reflog shows the pre-operation position, and git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD often works immediately, since Git sets ORIG_HEAD before risky operations; otherwise use the specific reflog entry such as HEAD@{5}. If you pushed a bad result, don't rewrite shared history: revert, or fix with a corrective commit.`,
    f: ["What is ORIG_HEAD?","When would you skip a commit during rebase?"]
  },
  62: {
    a: `The choice depends on whether the commits have been shared. git revert creates a new commit that applies the inverse of an earlier commit, leaving history intact. It's safe for shared branches because it only adds history: anyone who has pulled simply receives another commit. It also documents the undo in the log, which is good for audit and incident trails. Use revert for anything already pushed to a shared or protected branch such as main, production releases, or work others may have based changes on. git reset moves a branch pointer backward, effectively erasing commits from the branch's history; combined with force pushing, it rewrites shared history, which forces teammates to reconcile diverged branches, can lose their work, and breaks the audit trail. It's appropriate for local, unpushed commits: cleaning up before sharing, undoing a mistaken commit, or discarding experiments. Rule of thumb: local and private, reset or amend; public and shared, revert. Exceptions where reset plus force push on shared history is justified: removing a leaked secret or large accidental file, with coordination, communication, and temporary lifting of protection, and with rotation of the secret anyway. Nuances: reverting a merge commit needs -m to specify the parent, and re-merging later requires reverting the revert.`,
    f: ["Why is revert safer on shared branches?","When is a force push after reset justified?"]
  },
  63: {
    a: `Duplicate commits typically appear when someone rebases a branch that others have already merged or based work on, and then history gets reconciled with the old versions. For example, a teammate rebases a feature branch after you pulled it, force pushes, and you then merge your old copy with their rewritten one: both the old commits and their rebased equivalents, with different hashes but identical changes, now appear in history, often with conflicts or repeated changes. Diagnose: git log --oneline --graph shows pairs of commits with the same message and author; git cherry -v upstream branch lists commits and marks those whose patch-ids already exist upstream with a minus sign, identifying equivalent duplicates; git range-diff compares two versions of a series of commits. Cleanup options depend on whether the duplicates are pushed to a shared branch. If only on a feature branch and not yet merged to main: do an interactive rebase on top of the correct base and drop the duplicate commits, or use git rebase --onto to move only the unique commits, then force push with --force-with-lease after coordinating. Actually, plain git rebase usually detects and skips commits already upstream by patch-id.`,
    f: ["What does git cherry show?","How does rebase detect commits already upstream?"]
  },
  64: {
    a: `The reflog is Git's local journal of reference movements. It records every time HEAD or a branch tip changes: commits, checkouts, merges, rebases, resets, amends, cherry-picks, and pulls, each with the old and new hash, a timestamp, and a description. It lives in .git/logs: .git/logs/HEAD for HEAD movements, and .git/logs/refs/heads/<branch> and refs/remotes for individual branches, shown with git reflog or git reflog show <ref>. Entries are addressed as HEAD@{n} for the nth previous position, or with time expressions such as main@{2.days.ago}. It's local to each clone: it's not pushed, shared, or cloned, so it's useless for a fresh clone, and it's not a backup. It records only committed or checked-out states, not uncommitted work. Expiry: entries are pruned by git gc based on configuration, by default gc.reflogExpire of ninety days for entries still reachable from the current tip, and gc.reflogExpireUnreachable of thirty days for entries whose commits are unreachable from the tip. Therefore commits discarded by a reset or deleted branch are normally recoverable for at least thirty days, until pruned. You can tune these settings, or explicitly expire with git reflog expire, but doing so reduces your safety net.`,
    f: ["Is the reflog shared when you push?","How long do unreachable entries survive by default?"]
  },
  65: {
    a: `The two strategies reflect different release cultures. Trunk-based development has everyone integrate into main at least daily through very short-lived branches or direct commits, with unfinished work hidden behind feature flags, and releases cut from the trunk or its tags. It minimises merge pain, keeps integration continuous, and correlates with high delivery performance in DORA research. It requires a strong automated test suite, fast CI, feature flags, and discipline, plus a culture of small changes. GitFlow uses long-lived main and develop branches with feature, release, and hotfix branches; features merge to develop, releases are stabilised on a release branch, then merged to main and tagged. It offers clear structure for scheduled releases and maintaining multiple versions, but brings overhead, delayed integration, bigger merges, and confusion about where fixes belong. I'd choose trunk-based for web services and SaaS products with continuous or frequent deployment, microservices, and teams with good CI and testing; it fits a platform that deploys many times a day. I'd choose GitFlow, or a simplified release-branch model, for software distributed in versions that customers install, like mobile apps, on-premises products, and libraries needing parallel support of several versions, or for teams with heavy manual QA gates.`,
    f: ["Why does trunk-based development depend on feature flags?","Which fits software delivered as installable versions?"]
  },
  66: {
    a: `With 200 engineers in a monorepo, the first thing to break is performance and the second is process. Git operations slow with a huge number of files and history: clones take long, status and checkout lag. Mitigate with partial clone, sparse checkout, shallow clones in CI, the filesystem monitor and untracked cache, scheduled git maintenance with commit-graph, and tools like Scalar or a virtual filesystem. The main branch becomes a contention point: dozens of merges an hour cause constant rebases, and broken main from semantic conflicts. Use a merge queue that tests each pull request against the latest main plus queued changes before merging. CI cost and time explode if every change builds everything: adopt a build system with dependency graph awareness and remote caching, such as Bazel, Nx, Pants, or Turborepo, to build and test only affected targets, and path-based triggers. Ownership: use CODEOWNERS and directory-level responsibilities so reviews route correctly and sensitive areas have required reviewers. Access control is coarse; consider whether everyone can see all code, and add tooling if needed. Release and versioning: decide between a single version for everything and independent versions with tooling. Code health: shared libraries need compatibility policies and large-scale refactoring tools. Dependency management with a single version policy avoids diamond problems.`,
    f: ["What does a merge queue solve at this scale?","How do you avoid building everything on every change?"]
  },
  67: {
    a: `A merge queue, or merge train, is a feature that serialises merges to a protected branch so that each pull request is tested against the exact state that main will be in when it lands. Without a queue, two pull requests can each pass CI against the same main, but when both merge, the combination breaks, a semantic conflict, leaving main red. Requiring branches to be up to date before merging avoids this but forces everyone to repeatedly rebase and re-run CI when others merge first, which is slow and frustrating at scale. How a queue works: when a PR is approved and passes checks, the author adds it to the queue instead of merging. The system creates a temporary merge candidate branch combining main with that PR, and with the PRs ahead of it in the queue, runs CI on that combination, and if it passes, merges it; if it fails, it's removed and subsequent entries are re-tested without it. Many queues test in parallel speculatively to keep throughput up. Benefits: main stays green, no manual rebasing, fewer wasted CI runs, and developers can fire and forget. Costs: extra CI capacity and complexity, and a failing or flaky test blocks the queue, so keep tests reliable and fast.`,
    f: ["What is a semantic conflict?","How do queues keep throughput high?"]
  },
  68: {
    a: `A drifted long-running branch is costly to merge, so I'd manage it deliberately. First assess: how far has main moved, how many files conflict, and how much of the branch's work is still valid? Then choose a strategy. Sync incrementally: merge main into the branch, or rebase onto it, in steps rather than all at once; for example, merge main as of a week ago, then a few days later, resolving smaller conflicts each time and running tests at each step. Merging main into the branch is usually safer than rebasing a large, shared branch because it preserves history and doesn't require force pushing; rebasing is cleaner if it's mine alone and the commits are few. Enable rerere so repeated conflict resolutions are remembered. Use diff3 conflict style for context, talk to authors of conflicting changes, and run the full test suite after each sync. Consider reducing the size of the problem: break the branch into smaller pieces that can land independently, cherry-picking or splitting out refactorings, and use feature flags to merge incomplete work into main early. If it has diverged so much that merging is riskier than rewriting, port the changes onto a fresh branch from current main by re-applying the logic piece by piece.`,
    f: ["Why merge main into a shared long-running branch rather than rebase?","How does rerere help repeated syncs?"]
  },
  69: {
    a: `A safe hotfix workflow balances speed with control. Triage and decide: confirm severity, and consider whether a quick mitigation like a rollback or feature flag restores service faster than a code fix. If a fix is needed, branch from what's running in production, the release tag or production branch, not necessarily from main. Reproduce the issue with a failing test first, if possible, then implement the smallest fix, avoiding refactoring or unrelated changes. Open a pull request against the production branch, with a clear description of the incident and the fix; require at least one review, even if expedited, ideally by someone with context, and keep CI mandatory. Build the artefact once, tag it with a patch version, deploy to staging for a smoke test if time allows, then to production using the standard pipeline, ideally with a canary or staged rollout and a rollback plan ready. Monitor error rates, latency, and business metrics after deployment, and communicate status to stakeholders in the incident channel. Then merge or cherry-pick the fix back into main and any active release branches, so it isn't lost in the next release; verify with a check that the fix commit is reachable from main.`,
    f: ["Why backport the fix to main?","When is a rollback better than a hotfix?"]
  },
  70: {
    a: `A good PR is small, focused, and easy to review, and it carries the context a reviewer needs. Content: one logical change, ideally under a few hundred lines; a descriptive title; a description that explains why the change is needed, what approach was taken and alternatives considered, how it was tested, linked ticket, screenshots or logs for visible changes, and any deployment or rollback considerations. Passing CI before requesting review, with tests for new behaviour and no unrelated formatting noise. Self-review first, leaving comments on tricky parts. Keeping reviews fast and effective at scale. Set expectations: a review turnaround target, such as within one business day, and track time to first review. Route automatically with CODEOWNERS and review assignment rotation to spread the load. Automate the mechanical parts: linters, formatters, type checks, and security scans so humans focus on design, correctness, maintainability, and risk. Keep PRs small through stacked PRs, feature flags, and incremental delivery. Use draft PRs for early feedback on direction. Reviewers should be constructive, distinguish blocking issues from nits and suggestions, approve with comments when appropriate, and avoid blocking on style. Use PR templates and checklists. Encourage synchronous discussion for complex disagreements. Use merge queues to reduce rebasing overhead.`,
    f: ["How do you keep PRs small?","What should humans review versus linters?"]
  },
  71: {
    a: `Release versioning and tagging in an automated pipeline should be deterministic, traceable, and need little manual work. Adopt semantic versioning, MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH, so consumers can reason about compatibility. Derive version numbers from commit history using Conventional Commits: feat triggers a minor bump, fix a patch bump, and a breaking change marker a major bump. Tools such as semantic-release, release-please, GitVersion, or changesets for monorepos calculate the next version, update version files and the changelog, create an annotated tag, and publish a release with notes. Release-please opens a release pull request that, when merged, cuts the release, adding a human approval gate. The pipeline flow: merges to main run CI; the release job runs only on main after tests pass, determines the next version, tags the exact commit, builds the artefact once from that tag, tags the container image with the version and commit SHA, signs it, publishes to the registry, and creates the release notes. Deployments reference immutable versions or digests, never latest.`,
    f: ["How does release-please add a human gate?","Why embed the commit SHA in the build?"]
  },
  72: {
    a: `These three approaches share code between projects differently. Submodules embed another repository at a pinned commit, retaining its separate history and identity. Pros: exact source-level pinning, the subproject remains an independent repository you can push to, and you can see precisely what version is used. Cons: clumsy workflow, detached HEAD confusion, forgetting to initialise or push submodule commits, CI complexity, and hard for newcomers. Subtrees, git subtree, copy the other project's content into a subdirectory of your repository, with history merged in; you can pull updates and push changes back. Pros: no special clone steps, everything is in one repository, simple for consumers. Cons: bigger repository, history can get messy, and syncing upstream is manual, with commands that are less familiar. A package manager approach publishes the shared code as a versioned package to a registry such as npm, PyPI, Maven, or an internal artefact repository, and consumers declare a dependency version. Pros: clear versioning and release lifecycle, semantic versioning, caching, dependency resolution, and standard tooling and security scanning; consumers don't need source access. Cons: the overhead of publishing and releasing, and slower iteration when changing the library and consumer together.`,
    f: ["Why do packages usually beat submodules for shared libraries?","When is a subtree a good fit?"]
  },
  73: {
    a: `Enforcing commit conventions and quality gates requires layers, because local checks alone can be skipped. Local, for fast feedback: use a hook manager such as pre-commit, Husky, or Lefthook, with configuration committed to the repository so every developer gets the same hooks after one install step. Pre-commit hooks run formatters, linters, secret scanners, and quick tests on staged files; commit-msg hooks run commitlint to validate Conventional Commits; pre-push hooks run heavier checks. Hooks give quick feedback but can be bypassed with --no-verify or not installed, so they're a convenience, not enforcement. Authoritative checks go in CI: run the same linters, formatters in check mode, commitlint over all commits in the pull request, tests, and security scans as required status checks. Platform controls: branch protection requiring those checks, pull request reviews, and linear history or squash merge; when squashing, validate the PR title as the commit message with a check action, since that becomes the commit on main. Server-side hooks on self-hosted Git can reject pushes with invalid messages. Supporting measures: pull request and commit templates, documentation of conventions, editor integration, and setup scripts. Make hooks fast, as slow ones encourage bypassing; use incremental checks on changed files. Give clear error messages with fix instructions.`,
    f: ["Why can't pre-commit hooks be the only enforcement?","How do you validate commits when squash merging?"]
  },
  74: {
    a: `First diagnose why it's slow: is it history size, huge files, a large number of files, or heavy binaries? Use git count-objects -vH, git rev-list --objects --all with size analysis, or tools like git-sizer to find the culprits. Options. For clone and fetch time: shallow clones for automation, partial clones with --filter=blob:none to fetch content on demand, and single-branch clones. For working tree size: sparse checkout in cone mode to materialise only needed directories. For daily command speed: enable the filesystem monitor and untracked cache, core.fsmonitor, run git maintenance start for background commit-graph, prefetch, and repacking tasks, and consider Scalar, which configures these. For large binary files: move them out with Git LFS or into an artefact store. For bloated history: rewrite to remove large files or old junk using git filter-repo, which requires a coordinated migration since all hashes change, or archive old history and start a fresh repository with a grafted link to the old one. Server side: run garbage collection and repack, use bitmaps, and provide regional mirrors or caching proxies near CI runners. Structural options: split a monolith into multiple repositories if domains are independent, or adopt tooling suited to monorepos.`,
    f: ["How do you find what is making the repository large?","What are the trade-offs of rewriting history to shrink it?"]
  },
  75: {
    a: `Mapping branches to environments should avoid drift and keep promotion simple. A recommended approach for continuous delivery: one long-lived branch, main, representing the integrated state, and environments differing by configuration, not by branch. Every merge to main builds an immutable artefact tagged with the commit SHA; the pipeline deploys it automatically to dev, then promotes the same artefact to staging after tests, and to production after approval gates or automated checks, using environment-specific configuration injected at deploy time from separate files, Helm values, or Kustomize overlays. For GitOps, keep environment folders in a configuration repository, and promotion is a pull request or automated commit updating the version in the next environment's directory. Avoid branch-per-environment, such as dev, staging, and prod branches, because promotion means merging between them, which causes drift, cherry-pick confusion, and lets untested combinations reach production. Where a branch-based approach is mandated, keep merges strictly one-way and automated. Release-oriented models: for versioned software, use release branches such as release/2.3, deploy tags from them to production, and deploy main to dev and staging. Preview environments for pull requests, created and destroyed automatically, give early feedback. Protect production: required approvals, deployment windows, and environment protection rules.`,
    f: ["Why avoid a branch per environment?","How does promotion work in a GitOps layout?"]
  },
  76: {
    a: `A Git worktree is an additional working directory attached to the same repository, each with its own checked-out branch, index, and working files, but sharing the same object database and history. You create one with git worktree add ../review-pr pr-branch. How it helps in production and development workflows. Hotfixes without disruption: while in the middle of a feature with uncommitted changes, you can create a worktree from the production tag, fix the urgent bug, test, and push, with no stashing or context loss. Code review: check out a colleague's branch in a separate directory, run and test it while keeping your own work open. Long-running tasks: run a slow test suite or build on one branch while continuing to work on another. Comparing versions side by side, for debugging regressions, or running two versions of an application simultaneously. Parallel release maintenance: keep worktrees for each supported release branch to apply backports. Automation and tooling: CI agents or AI coding agents can each work in isolated worktrees without conflicting. Compared with multiple clones, worktrees save disk space and network, since they share objects, and commits and branches are instantly visible across them.`,
    f: ["Why can't the same branch be in two worktrees?","How do worktrees help with parallel hotfix work?"]
  },
  77: {
    a: `Enforcing review and approval policy relies on platform branch protection or rulesets, backed by culture and tooling. Configure protection on main and release branches: require pull requests, with no direct pushes, including for administrators where possible; require a minimum number of approving reviews, typically one or two, higher for sensitive repositories; dismiss stale approvals when new commits are pushed; require approval from someone other than the last pusher, so authors can't approve their own changes; require review from code owners for protected paths defined in CODEOWNERS, such as infrastructure, authentication, and CI workflow files; require all conversations to be resolved; require status checks, including build, test, linters, and security scans, and require branches to be up to date or use a merge queue; require signed commits if provenance matters; restrict who can push, merge, or bypass; block force pushes and deletions; and require linear history if preferred. Use organisation-level rulesets so all repositories receive the baseline, and apply them with automation or Terraform to avoid drift. Deployment protection: environment rules requiring approval from named reviewers before production deployments. Audit: monitor changes to protection rules and bypass events in audit logs. Cultural side: define review guidelines, response time expectations, and training. Provide a documented emergency bypass with post-hoc review.`,
    f: ["Why block authors from approving their own pull requests?","How do you apply the baseline to every repository?"]
  },
  78: {
    a: `Commit signing attaches a cryptographic signature to commits and tags, proving they were created by the holder of a particular key and haven't been altered. Without it, author names and emails are unauthenticated text, so anyone can forge commits claiming to be someone else. Why: integrity and provenance for the software supply chain, guarding against impersonation after a stolen token, compliance and audit requirements, and trust in release tags. How: Git supports GPG, SSH keys, and X.509 certificates, and Sigstore's gitsign offers keyless signing. For GPG: generate a key, tell Git with user.signingkey, enable commit.gpgsign true and tag.gpgsign true, and upload the public key to the hosting platform. For SSH signing, simpler since developers already have keys: set gpg.format ssh, point user.signingkey to the public key, and add it to the platform as a signing key; allowed signers files enable local verification. Hardware security keys protect private keys. Verification: git log --show-signature, git verify-commit, and the platform's Verified badge. Enforcement: branch protection rules requiring signed commits on protected branches, so unsigned pushes are rejected; CI or admission checks verifying signatures on release tags; web-based merges are signed by the platform's own key.`,
    f: ["What is the difference between GPG and SSH signing?","What does a Verified badge not guarantee?"]
  },
  79: {
    a: `GitOps is an operating model where the desired state of infrastructure and applications is declared in Git, and automated agents continuously reconcile the real environment to match it. The four principles: the system is described declaratively; the desired state is versioned and immutable in Git; approved changes are applied automatically; and software agents ensure correctness and alert on drift. Git becomes the source of truth. All changes, whether an image version bump, a scaling change, or a firewall rule, go through pull requests, with review, CI validation, and policy checks, and the merged commit is the record. An agent such as Argo CD or Flux running in the cluster pulls from the repository and applies changes, and continuously compares live state with Git, reverting or flagging manual changes. For infrastructure, tools like Terraform driven by Atlantis or pipelines, or Crossplane, apply the same idea. Benefits: full audit trail of who changed what and why, easy rollback by reverting a commit, consistent environments, disaster recovery by re-applying the repository to a new cluster, less need for cluster credentials outside the cluster, and a familiar developer workflow.`,
    f: ["How does the agent detect and correct drift?","What happens to secrets in a GitOps repository?"]
  },
  80: {
    a: `A personal access token is tied to a human's identity, and typically carries broad permissions across everything that user can access. Using one in CI creates several problems. Security: it's long-lived, so if leaked from a log, a misconfigured job, or a compromised third-party action, an attacker gets the user's access until someone notices and revokes it. It often has more scope than the pipeline needs, violating least privilege. Lifecycle fragility: when the employee leaves or their account is deactivated, pipelines suddenly break, or worse, tokens of departed employees remain active. Accountability: actions in the audit logs appear as that person, blurring who or what actually did it. Shared use of one personal token across many jobs prevents attribution and makes rotation painful. What replaces it. The CI platform's automatically issued, short-lived job token, such as GitHub's GITHUB_TOKEN with explicit minimal permissions, or GitLab's CI job token, scoped to the repository and expiring when the job ends. For cross-repository access, use GitHub Apps or fine-grained tokens/service accounts with narrowly scoped, repository-specific permissions, installed at the organisation level, generating short-lived installation tokens. Read-only deploy keys for single-repository checkouts. For cloud access, OIDC federation instead of stored keys.`,
    f: ["What does the job token's scope look like?","Why does a GitHub App beat a personal token for automation?"]
  },
  81: {
    a: `Once a secret is in Git history, you must assume it's compromised, so the aim is to stop it getting there, using overlapping barriers. Design so there's less to leak: use secret managers, workload identity, and short-lived credentials, and provide a template with configuration read from the environment at runtime. Developer side: .gitignore entries for env files, key files, and local config, a committed .env.example with placeholders, and pre-commit hooks running gitleaks, detect-secrets, or TruffleHog on staged changes, distributed through a hook framework with a committed configuration. Remote side, which can't be bypassed locally: platform push protection, such as GitHub's, rejects pushes containing recognised secret formats; server-side pre-receive hooks on self-hosted servers do the same. Pull request side: required CI checks scanning the diff and commit messages, failing the build on findings. Continuous: organisation-wide secret scanning of existing repositories, including full history, with alerts routed to owners and validity checks for detected credentials. Cultural: training, easy ways to get secrets properly, and a blameless reporting path so people disclose mistakes quickly. Tuning: maintain custom patterns for internal token formats, manage allowlists with expiry, and track false-positive rates, as noisy tools get disabled. Have a rehearsed response: revoke, rotate, investigate, then clean history. The combination makes leaks rare and quickly contained.`,
    f: ["Why isn't a pre-commit hook enough by itself?","What do you do when push protection blocks a legitimate test value?"]
  },
  82: {
    a: `A Git-triggered deployment pipeline starts with events from the repository. A webhook or native integration sends push, pull request, and tag events to the CI system. On pull request: run validation: lint, unit tests, build, security scans, and IaC plan, report status checks to the pull request, and optionally create a preview environment. On merge to main: build the artefact once, tag it with the commit SHA, run integration tests, scan, sign, and publish to the registry; then deploy automatically to a development or staging environment and run smoke or end-to-end tests. Promotion to production is triggered by a tag, a manual approval in an environment protection rule, or an automated gate based on checks and metrics. Deployment can be push-based, where the pipeline runs helm or kubectl using short-lived credentials from OIDC, or pull-based GitOps, where the pipeline updates a configuration repository and Argo CD or Flux applies it. Use progressive delivery, such as canary or blue-green, with health checks and automatic rollback on alert. Controls: protected branches and required reviews, pinned and trusted actions, least-privilege secrets per environment, concurrency control so only one deployment runs per environment at a time, path filters for monorepos, and audit logs linking deployments to commits.`,
    f: ["What triggers production promotion?","How does GitOps change the deployment step?"]
  },
  83: {
    a: `A signed or verified release is an artefact, such as a tag, binary, package, or container image, that carries a cryptographic signature proving who produced it and that it hasn't been modified since. Consumers verify the signature before trusting it. In supply-chain security, this addresses tampering and impersonation between build and use. Tools: GPG-signed tags, Sigstore cosign for images and binaries with keyless signing tied to the CI identity, and registries or package ecosystems that support signatures. SLSA, Supply-chain Levels for Software Artifacts, goes further by defining the assurance level of the build process itself. A key element is provenance: a signed attestation recording what source repository and commit, which builder, and what build steps produced the artefact. At SLSA build level 1 you have provenance; level 2 requires a hosted build platform that signs it, so it's hard to forge; level 3 requires a hardened, isolated builder so that even compromised build steps or maintainers can't tamper with provenance. A release pipeline therefore builds on a hosted runner, generates an SBOM and provenance, signs the artefact and the attestations, records the signing event in a transparency log, and publishes them together.`,
    f: ["What does provenance record?","What does SLSA level 3 add over level 2?"]
  },
  84: {
    a: `Git hooks are executable scripts that Git runs at specific points, found in .git/hooks, or in a directory set by core.hooksPath. Client-side hooks run on a developer's machine: pre-commit before a commit is created, for linting, formatting, and secret scanning; prepare-commit-msg and commit-msg for templating or validating messages; pre-push for heavier checks like tests; pre-rebase; and post-checkout, post-merge, and post-commit for housekeeping such as installing dependencies. A non-zero exit from the pre-hooks aborts the operation. Server-side hooks run on the receiving repository: pre-receive runs once for a push and can reject the whole push based on policy, such as blocking force pushes, enforcing commit message formats, or scanning for secrets or large files; update runs per branch being updated; post-receive runs after acceptance, to trigger notifications, CI, or deployment. Client-side hooks aren't versioned or enforced: they live outside the tracked files, require each developer to install them, and can be bypassed with --no-verify, so they're for fast, friendly feedback. Server-side hooks are authoritative because every push must pass them, though on hosted platforms such as GitHub they're replaced by branch protection, required status checks, push protection, and webhooks.`,
    f: ["Which hook can reject an entire push?","Why can't client hooks be relied upon for policy?"]
  },
  85: {
    a: `If a dependency was compromised via a malicious commit, I need to determine what changed, when, by whom, and what it affected in my systems. Scope: identify the compromised repository, the affected versions or commit range, and the advisory details if one exists. Investigate in Git: use git log with date and author filters on the dependency's history, git show for the suspect commits, and git diff between the last known-good tag and the compromised one, looking for added network calls, obfuscated code, install scripts, changed build or CI files, new maintainers or unusual signing. Check commit metadata: authorship versus committer mismatches, signature verification status, odd timestamps, and whether the commit arrived via a pull request or direct push; git blame and log on changed files show prior history. Check whether released artefacts match the source: compare the published package with the tagged source, as attackers sometimes change only the published artefact. Then assess my exposure: find which of my repositories and builds depend on the affected versions, using lockfiles in Git history, git log -S on the lockfile to see when the version was introduced, and SBOMs. Determine whether affected builds ran and where it was deployed.`,
    f: ["How does git log -S help trace when a version was introduced?","Why compare the published package with the source?"]
  },
  86: {
    a: `In a Git-driven deployment, the migration scripts are versioned with the code and deployed by the pipeline, so safety depends on process and design. Keep migrations in the repository as ordered, immutable files managed by a tool like Flyway, Liquibase, Alembic, or the framework's migrator, and never edit one that has been applied. Design every change as backward compatible with expand and contract, since during a rolling deployment both the old and new application versions run against the same schema: add columns or tables first, deploy code that uses them, backfill data in batches, and remove old structures in a later release. Run migrations as a single, controlled pipeline step before the application rollout, not at startup of each replica, using a lock so only one runs. Validate in CI by applying all migrations to a fresh database and to a production-like copy, measuring duration and locks; lint risky operations. Use lock timeouts and online techniques, such as concurrent index creation, and batched updates for large tables. Gate production with review of the migration pull request, especially destructive operations, and take a snapshot or backup first. Plan rollback as roll forward or application rollback, since reversing schema changes is risky; ensure old code remains compatible.`,
    f: ["Why must migrations be backward compatible in rolling deploys?","Why not run migrations on every pod start?"]
  },
  87: {
    a: `git fsck, file system check, verifies the integrity and connectivity of the object database. It checks that objects are well-formed and match their hashes, that references point to existing objects, that trees reference valid blobs and subtrees, that commits reference valid trees and parents, and it reports problems such as corrupt, missing, or malformed objects, broken links, and dangling objects. Dangling objects are unreachable objects, like commits left after a reset or deleted branch, which isn't an error but is useful for recovery. Useful options: --full checks packfiles and alternates, --strict is stricter about format issues, --unreachable lists objects not reachable from any reference, --lost-found writes dangling commits and blobs to .git/lost-found for recovery, and --connectivity-only skips content checks for speed. Integrity verification use: after suspected disk problems, interrupted operations, or a crash; before and after migrating or repacking repositories; when a clone or fetch complains about missing or bad objects; as a scheduled check on servers hosting repositories; and in forensic work to verify that history hasn't been tampered with. Because object IDs are content hashes, fsck effectively verifies the whole Merkle structure. Settings like transfer.fsckObjects, fetch.fsckObjects, and receive.fsckObjects make Git validate objects during transfers, rejecting malformed data.`,
    f: ["What is a dangling object?","What does transfer.fsckObjects protect against?"]
  },
  88: {
    a: `Automatic rollback tied to Git means that when a deployment is unhealthy, the system returns to the last known-good commit or version without manual steps, and Git records it. Components. Immutable, versioned artefacts tagged by commit SHA, so the previous good version is always available. Health signals: readiness probes, smoke tests, and monitoring metrics such as error rate and latency compared with a baseline, evaluated for a bake period after deployment. A controller that watches those signals: Argo Rollouts or Flagger analyse canary metrics from Prometheus and automatically abort and revert traffic or replica sets when thresholds are exceeded; Kubernetes alone stops a rollout if new pods don't become ready but needs a progress deadline plus an external trigger to undo. For GitOps, the rollback must be reflected in Git so the declared state matches reality, otherwise Argo CD would reapply the bad version: so the pipeline or a bot creates a revert commit in the configuration repository, or pins the previous version, and the agent syncs it. Alternatively, pause automated sync and use the tool's rollback to a known revision while a revert is prepared. In push-based pipelines, a failure step runs a rollback job redeploying the previous digest and opens an incident.`,
    f: ["Why must the rollback be reflected in Git?","What limits automatic rollback when migrations are involved?"]
  },
  89: {
    a: `Ephemeral preview environments are temporary, isolated deployments created automatically for each pull request, so reviewers and testers can see and use the change running live before it merges. Git drives the lifecycle through events: when a pull request is opened or updated, the pipeline builds the branch's artefact, tagged with the commit SHA, and deploys it to a uniquely named environment, for example a Kubernetes namespace pr-1234, with a unique URL like pr-1234.preview.example.com; it posts the link as a comment on the pull request. New pushes redeploy it. When the pull request is merged or closed, a cleanup job destroys the environment and its resources. Implementation approaches: a CI workflow using Helm or Kustomize with the PR number as a variable; Argo CD ApplicationSets with the pull request generator, which creates and removes an Application for each open PR automatically; or platform features like Vercel, Netlify, and Render previews. Considerations: data, using seeded, anonymised, or ephemeral databases rather than production data; shared dependencies, either mocked or pointed to a shared dev stack; secrets, scoped and minimal; cost control, with resource limits, automatic expiry, and scale-to-zero; wildcard DNS and TLS certificates; authentication so previews aren't public; and security for fork PRs, which shouldn't receive secrets.`,
    f: ["How does Argo CD's pull request generator work?","How do you control cost for preview environments?"]
  },
  90: {
    a: `Compliance auditing requires reliable answers to who changed what, when, why, and who approved it. Git itself provides much of this: commit history records author, committer, timestamp, message, and diff, and signed commits prove identity. git log with file and author filters, and blame, answer who changed a line. But Git metadata alone can be forged or rewritten, so you need controls around it. Protect history: branch protection blocks force pushes and deletions on main, so history is immutable in practice. Enforce review: required pull requests with approvals, code owner review, and status checks, so every change has an independent approver and passing tests; the pull request records discussion, approvers, and linked tickets. Require signed commits for non-repudiation. Platform audit logs record administrative actions: access changes, permission changes, branch protection edits, token creation, repository visibility changes, and force-push attempts; stream these to a SIEM with long retention. Link changes to work items by requiring ticket IDs in commit messages or PR titles. For deployments, connect commits to what ran in production through artefact tags and deployment records, so you can trace production back to approved changes. Evidence collection: automate reports of changes in a period, approvals, and exceptions, to satisfy SOC 2 or ISO 27001 auditors.`,
    f: ["Why isn't Git history alone sufficient evidence?","How do you trace production back to approved changes?"]
  },
  91: {
    a: `Platforms offer three ways to integrate a pull request, each producing a different history. A merge commit preserves every commit from the branch and adds a merge commit with two parents. It keeps the full, true history, including the branch structure, but main becomes non-linear and noisy with 'fix typo' commits and merge bubbles; reverting the whole feature means reverting the merge with -m 1. A squash merge collapses all the branch's commits into one new commit on main, whose message is usually the PR title and description. History is linear, with one commit per pull request, easy to read, bisect, and revert; but individual commits and their granularity are lost, authorship becomes the PR author with co-author trailers, and later branches stacked on the original will conflict. A rebase merge replays the branch's commits on top of main individually, without a merge commit, creating new commits with new hashes: linear history while retaining the individual commits. It requires well-crafted, atomic commits, and it rewrites hashes, so the commits on main differ from those on the branch and signatures are not preserved.`,
    f: ["Which strategy loses individual commit granularity?","Why does a rebase merge change commit hashes?"]
  },
  92: {
    a: `Across many repositories, access control must be systematic. Principles. Authenticate centrally with SSO and enforce MFA; manage membership through your identity provider's groups synced to teams on the Git platform, so joiners, movers, and leavers are handled automatically. Grant access to teams, never individuals, with roles like read, triage, write, maintain, and admin, assigned at the lowest necessary level; most people need read or write, very few need admin. Base permissions for the organisation should be none or read, with write granted per repository through teams. Use rulesets or organisation-level policies to apply the same branch protection baseline everywhere, so nobody can push directly to main, and CODEOWNERS for sensitive paths. Control tokens and apps: disable classic personal access tokens, prefer fine-grained tokens and GitHub Apps with minimal, repository-scoped permissions, and review installed OAuth apps and deploy keys periodically. Restrict who can create repositories, change visibility, or fork private repositories. Separate duties: production deployment credentials and environment approvals owned by distinct groups. Automate: manage repository settings and team access as code with Terraform or a settings-as-code tool, so drift is detected and changes are reviewed. Review: scheduled access recertification, removal of inactive accounts and stale deploy keys, and audit log monitoring for privilege changes, new tokens, and bulk cloning.`,
    f: ["Why assign access to teams instead of individuals?","How do you manage repository settings as code?"]
  },
  93: {
    a: `A three-way merge combines two versions of a file by using their common ancestor as a reference. When merging branches A and B, Git finds the merge base, the most recent common ancestor commit, and compares each side against it. For each region of the file: if only A changed it, take A's change; if only B changed it, take B's; if both made identical changes, take that; if neither changed it, keep the original; and if both changed the same region in different ways, that's a conflict, and Git inserts conflict markers for a human to resolve. The ancestor is what makes this smarter than a two-way comparison, which can't tell who changed what, and would show every difference as conflict. Git's default strategy for merging two branches is ort, which replaced recursive; it handles renames by detecting similar files, and when there are multiple merge bases, as in criss-cross merges, it merges the bases to create a virtual ancestor. The same three-way logic powers rebase and cherry-pick, where the commit's parent plays the base role.`,
    f: ["Why does the common ancestor matter?","What is a criss-cross merge?"]
  },
  94: {
    a: `For a complex conflict I work methodically rather than editing markers hastily. First, understand the context: git status shows the conflicted files; git log --merge --oneline shows the commits on each side touching them; and git diff --name-only --diff-filter=U lists unresolved paths. Set merge.conflictStyle to zdiff3 so markers include the common ancestor. For each file, understand what each side was trying to achieve, by reading the commit messages, linked pull requests, and talking to the other author if the intent is unclear. Sometimes it's best to resolve in stages: handle the mechanical, renamed, or moved parts first, then the semantic ones; for big conflicts, merge main into the branch in smaller increments. Use a three-pane merge tool, with git mergetool, showing local, remote, base, and result. Resolve by combining intent rather than picking sides, and use git checkout --ours or --theirs only when one side should win wholesale, remembering these names swap in a rebase. For generated files or lockfiles, regenerate them after resolving the source rather than hand-editing. After resolving, remove all markers, build, run tests and linters, and review the final diff with git diff against both parents. Stage and continue the operation. If it's becoming a mess, abort and rethink. Enable rerere so repeated resolutions are reused.`,
    f: ["What do ours and theirs mean during a rebase?","Why regenerate lockfiles instead of hand-merging?"]
  },
  95: {
    a: `git rerere, reuse recorded resolution, remembers how you resolved a particular conflict and automatically applies the same resolution when it sees the same conflict again. Enable it with git config rerere.enabled true. When a conflict occurs, Git records the preimage, the conflicted state; when you resolve and commit, it records the postimage, the resolution, keyed by a hash of the conflict. On later encounters of an identical conflict, Git applies the recorded resolution automatically, though you still need to stage and review the result. Cases where it pays off. Long-running branches that you repeatedly rebase or merge main into: the same conflicts reappear at each sync, and rerere resolves them once. Rebasing a stack of commits, where the same conflict recurs at several commits. Trying a merge, aborting, and retrying; testing a merge in a throwaway branch to learn the resolution, then redoing it for real; maintainers who carry a topic branch across many upstream updates; and when rebase is redone after rewording or reordering.`,
    f: ["Where are recorded resolutions stored?","How do you undo a bad recorded resolution?"]
  },
  96: {
    a: `Git stores objects in two forms. Loose objects are individual files in .git/objects, named by hash, each zlib-compressed; every new blob, tree, and commit starts life this way. They're simple and quick to write but inefficient at scale: many small files, no cross-object compression. Packfiles store many objects in a single file with an accompanying index for fast lookup. Inside a pack, similar objects are stored as deltas against a base object, so successive versions of a file take little space; Git chooses delta bases heuristically, often by file name and size. Packs are created when you push or fetch, since transfers use packs, and by git gc and git repack. What git gc does: packs loose objects into packfiles, consolidates multiple packs, compresses with deltas, packs references into the packed-refs file, removes unreachable objects older than the expiry, normally two weeks, expires old reflog entries, and updates auxiliary data such as the commit-graph. git gc --auto runs automatically when there are too many loose objects or packs, and servers should schedule git maintenance. Why it matters: repository size on disk, clone and fetch performance, and operation speed. Aggressive repacking is slower but yields smaller packs.`,
    f: ["How do packfiles save space?","What do loose objects look like on disk?"]
  },
  97: {
    a: `Git handles binary files poorly for two reasons: it stores a full copy of each version, since binaries rarely delta-compress well, so the repository balloons with every change, and it cannot merge them, so concurrent edits cause unresolvable conflicts. Every clone also downloads all historical versions. Git LFS, Large File Storage, solves the size problem by storing large files outside the repository's object database. Under the hood it uses Git's clean and smudge filters, configured via .gitattributes patterns set by git lfs track. When you git add a tracked file, the clean filter replaces the file's content with a small text pointer file, containing the LFS spec version, the sha256 oid of the content, and the size; the real content is saved in the local .git/lfs/objects cache. On git push, a pre-push hook uploads the actual content to the LFS server over an HTTP API, while the pointer goes into Git history. On checkout, the smudge filter reads the pointer, downloads the content from the LFS server if it isn't cached, and puts the real file in the working tree. So history remains small, and clones fetch only the large files needed for the commit being checked out.`,
    f: ["What do the clean and smudge filters do?","What does a pointer file look like?"]
  },
  98: {
    a: `The commit-graph file is a precomputed, compact binary cache, stored in .git/objects/info/commit-graph, of the commit history structure: for each commit it holds the commit's parents, root tree ID, commit date, and a generation number, in an efficient format. Normally, to traverse history, Git has to read and decompress each commit object from the object database, which is slow in large repositories with hundreds of thousands of commits. With the commit-graph, walking history reads this compact file instead, speeding up commands that traverse the graph, such as git log with graph and filters, git merge-base, reachability checks like git branch --contains and git tag --contains, and fetch and push negotiation. The generation number, a value derived from the longest path to a root, makes reachability queries much faster because Git can stop walking early when a commit's generation is lower than the target's. Newer versions also support changed-path Bloom filters, which record which paths each commit modified, greatly accelerating git log -- path and blame. Writing: git commit-graph write --reachable, or automatically via fetch.writeCommitGraph and gc.writeCommitGraph; git maintenance schedules incremental updates. Benefits are most visible on large monorepos and servers; hosting platforms rely on it heavily. Verify with git commit-graph verify.`,
    f: ["What do Bloom filters in the commit-graph speed up?","What is a generation number?"]
  },
  99: {
    a: `When merging, Git can fast-forward if the target branch hasn't diverged: the target's tip is an ancestor of the branch being merged, so Git just moves the pointer forward, with no merge commit, producing linear history in which the branch's commits appear as if made directly on main. git merge --no-ff forces a merge commit even when a fast-forward is possible, creating an explicit commit with two parents that records that a branch existed and was integrated. Why use no-ff. It preserves the grouping of a feature's commits under one merge commit, so you can see where the feature started and ended, and revert the entire feature with a single git revert -m 1 on the merge commit. It documents integration points, helpful for release notes and audits, with the pull request referenced in the merge message. Why use fast-forward. It keeps history linear and simple, free from clutter, and is natural when the branch has been rebased onto main beforehand; git merge --ff-only refuses to create merge commits, ensuring linear history and failing if main moved, which can be a protective default through merge.ff only. GitFlow's tradition is no-ff for feature merges; trunk-based teams generally prefer linear history using squash or rebase merges.`,
    f: ["What does --ff-only protect against?","How does no-ff make reverting a feature easier?"]
  },
  100: {
    a: `Internally, git stash saves your local modifications as commits and stores a reference to them. When you run git stash, Git creates a commit capturing the index state, with HEAD as its parent, and a second commit capturing the working tree, with both HEAD and the index commit as parents, so the stash is actually a merge-like commit; with -u, a third commit holds untracked files. The reference refs/stash points to the latest stash commit, and older entries are kept in the reflog of refs/stash, which is why stash@{n} syntax matches reflog syntax. After saving, Git resets the working tree and index to HEAD. git stash apply or pop does a three-way merge to reapply the changes, using the original base commit as the common ancestor, which means it works even if the branch has moved on, with potential conflicts. Pitfalls. Stashes are local and not pushed, so they're easily forgotten and not backed up. Untracked and ignored files aren't stashed by default, needing -u or -a. Pop on conflict doesn't drop the stash, leaving it on the list, which can confuse; always check. Old stashes become hard to apply as code drifts. They aren't tied to a branch, so you may apply to the wrong one.`,
    f: ["What are the three commits in a stash?","Why does a conflicting pop keep the stash?"]
  },
  101: {
    a: `A refspec tells Git how to map references between a remote and the local repository. The format is [+]<src>:<dst>. In fetch, src is the remote ref and dst the local ref where it is stored; the default fetch refspec in .git/config is +refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*, meaning that every branch on origin is fetched into a remote-tracking branch under refs/remotes/origin, with the plus sign allowing non-fast-forward updates of the tracking ref. In push, src is the local ref and dst is the remote ref: git push origin main:release pushes local main to the remote branch release; git push origin :old-branch, with an empty source, deletes the remote branch; git push origin HEAD:refs/heads/feature pushes the current commit regardless of the name. Wildcards allow patterns. You can customise fetch behaviour by adding refspecs: for example fetching GitHub pull request heads with +refs/pull/*/head:refs/remotes/origin/pr/*, which makes every pull request available locally as a branch, useful for review tooling and CI; or limiting the fetch to one branch for speed. Tags have their own default refspec behaviour: refs/tags/*:refs/tags/*. Settings such as push.default and remote.<name>.push define defaults. Understanding refspecs explains the confusing parts of Git: why origin/main is a separate local reference, how to mirror repositories with git push --mirror or +refs/*:refs/*, and how to delete or rename remote branches.`,
    f: ["What does the + in a refspec mean?","How do you fetch pull request heads?"]
  },
  102: {
    a: `A fork is your own copy of someone else's repository on the hosting platform; keeping it current with upstream involves adding the original repository as a second remote. Setup: git remote add upstream <original-url>, with origin pointing to your fork; verify with git remote -v. To sync: git fetch upstream to retrieve the latest changes, then integrate into your local main. For a clean, linear approach when you don't commit directly to main: git switch main then git merge --ff-only upstream/main, or git reset --hard upstream/main if you want to discard divergence, then git push origin main to update your fork. Platforms also offer a sync fork button or API call. For feature branches on your fork, bring in upstream changes by rebasing: git rebase upstream/main, which replays your commits on top of the latest upstream, resolving conflicts as needed, then push with --force-with-lease since history changed; or merge upstream/main into the branch if you prefer to avoid rewriting. Best practices: never commit directly to your fork's main; do work on topic branches, so main can always fast-forward to upstream; sync before starting new work and before opening a pull request; keep pull requests small and up to date; and enable fetch.prune.`,
    f: ["Why avoid committing directly to the fork's main?","How do you rebase a feature branch onto upstream?"]
  },
  103: {
    a: `git replace and git notes are two lesser-known mechanisms. git replace creates a replacement reference that makes Git substitute one object for another when reading history, without rewriting commits or changing their hashes. For example, git replace <old-commit> <new-commit> stores a ref under refs/replace/. Everywhere Git reads the old object, it sees the new one instead. Scenario: grafting history together, such as joining a new repository to the archived history of a legacy system, by replacing the first commit of the new repository with a commit whose parent is the last commit of the old history, giving a continuous log without rewriting. Another is temporarily fixing a commit's content or parentage to test what a rewrite would look like, before running filter-repo to make it permanent. Replace refs aren't pushed by default, so collaborators won't see them unless they fetch refs/replace. git notes attach extra information to existing commits without altering them, stored in a separate notes ref, refs/notes/commits, keyed by commit hash. Scenario: CI results, code review links, deployment records, or audit annotations added after the fact, since commit messages are immutable. For instance, a deployment system adds a note 'deployed to prod on this date' to the commit, visible in git log.`,
    f: ["Why would you graft history with git replace?","Why add information as a note rather than amend the commit?"]
  },
  104: {
    a: `An octopus merge is a merge commit with more than two parents, created when merging three or more branches at once, such as git merge feature-a feature-b feature-c. It's Git's strategy for combining several heads in one operation, and the resulting merge commit has multiple parents. Limits: the octopus strategy only works if all the merges can be done automatically; if any conflicts arise, Git refuses and aborts, as it cannot ask a human to resolve in the middle of a multi-way merge. So it suits cases where the branches touch independent areas. Where it's used: the Linux kernel has some famous large octopus merges, and some maintainers merge many topic branches into an integration branch in one step; it can also be used in release preparation to combine independent topic branches neatly, or to keep history tidy when merging many small, unrelated changes. Whether to use it: for most teams, rarely. History with many-parent merges is hard to read and reason about, tools and visualisations handle them poorly, reverting one parent's changes out of an octopus is awkward since git revert -m selects only one parent, and bisecting through them is trickier. Sequential merges are clearer and let you resolve conflicts at each step.`,
    f: ["Why does an octopus merge abort on conflicts?","Why is reverting part of an octopus awkward?"]
  },
  105: {
    a: `Migrating a large legacy repository means first understanding what's wrong, then choosing a migration approach that minimises disruption. Assess: measure size and growth with git-sizer and git count-objects, and find the largest blobs and directories, long-lost binaries, vendored dependencies, generated files, committed secrets, and unwanted history. Define the goals: smaller clones, a restructured layout, split into several repositories, or merge into a monorepo. Tooling: git filter-repo is the recommended way to rewrite history: remove large files or paths, extract a subdirectory into its own repository with history using --subdirectory-filter or --path, rename directories, or rewrite author information with a mailmap; BFG is a simpler alternative for removing files and secrets. Move big binaries to LFS with git lfs migrate. To merge multiple repositories into a monorepo while preserving history, rewrite each into a subdirectory with filter-repo and merge with --allow-unrelated-histories, or use git subtree.`,
    f: ["What does git filter-repo produce that helps after migration?","What are the alternatives to rewriting history?"]
  },
  106: {
    a: `A textual merge conflict is when Git cannot automatically combine changes because two branches modified the same lines or one deleted what the other edited. Git detects it, stops, and asks a human. A semantic conflict is when the merge is textually clean, so Git succeeds without complaint, but the combined result is logically broken, for example one branch renames a function while another adds a new call to the old name, or two changes each valid separately violate an invariant together. Git cannot detect these, because it works on text, not meaning. Defences against textual conflicts: keep branches short-lived and merge or rebase frequently; make small, focused changes; communicate who is working on which areas, using CODEOWNERS and ownership; avoid sweeping reformatting mixed with logic changes, and run formatters before; modularise code so changes touch different files; use rerere, merge tools, and diff3 style for resolution.`,
    f: ["Why can Git not detect semantic conflicts?","How does a merge queue prevent them?"]
  },
  107: {
    a: `HEAD is a special reference that identifies your current position in the repository: the commit that will become the parent of your next commit, and it determines what's checked out in your working tree. It normally contains the name of a branch, such as ref: refs/heads/main, a symbolic reference, so HEAD points to a branch, which points to a commit. When you commit, Git creates the commit and advances the branch that HEAD points to. Switching branches changes what HEAD refers to. Related references: HEAD~1 or HEAD^ is the first parent, HEAD~2 the grandparent, and HEAD@{1} is where HEAD was previously, from the reflog. Detached HEAD is the state in which HEAD contains a commit hash directly instead of a branch name. This happens when you check out a tag, a specific commit, or a remote-tracking branch such as origin/main, and also during bisect and in the middle of rebases. In this state you can look around and even commit, but new commits aren't on any branch; if you then switch away, those commits become unreachable, recoverable only through the reflog until garbage collection. Git warns you on entering it. To keep work done in a detached HEAD, create a branch there with git switch -c name.`,
    f: ["What is the difference between HEAD~1 and HEAD@{1}?","How do you keep commits made while detached?"]
  },
  108: {
    a: `git checkout historically did several unrelated things, which made it confusing: switching branches, creating branches with -b, checking out a commit into a detached HEAD, and restoring files from the index or a commit. In Git 2.23, two newer commands split these responsibilities. git switch is for changing branches: git switch main, git switch -c new-branch to create and switch, git switch --detach <commit> for a deliberate detached HEAD, and git switch - to go back to the previous branch. It refuses to do file restores, so it's safer and clearer. git restore is for restoring file contents: git restore file discards working tree changes by taking the version from the index; git restore --staged file unstages; git restore --source=<commit> file brings a file from a specific commit into the working tree; and --staged --worktree restores both. Compared to checkout, which could ambiguously treat an argument as either a branch or a path, requiring '--' to disambiguate, these have clear semantics. checkout still works and remains in countless tutorials and scripts, so it's good to know both. Danger: git restore on working tree files permanently discards uncommitted changes, just as checkout -- file did.`,
    f: ["What ambiguity did git checkout have?","How do you unstage a file with the new commands?"]
  },
  109: {
    a: `The staging area, or index, is a snapshot of what will go into your next commit. It sits between the working tree and the repository: you modify files, add the ones you want with git add, and commit the staged snapshot. Technically it's a binary file, .git/index, listing paths, file modes, blob hashes, and metadata for fast change detection. Why it exists. Crafting commits: you can stage only some files, or even individual hunks with git add -p, so each commit is a logical, reviewable unit even when your working tree contains several unrelated changes. It enables clean history without forcing you to work in tiny steps. Review before committing: git diff --staged shows exactly what you're about to commit. Merge support: during a merge, the index holds up to three versions of conflicted files, the base, ours, and theirs, in stages one to three, and tracks which conflicts are resolved. Performance: stat information in the index lets Git quickly detect which files changed without hashing everything. It also provides a place to build the tree object for the commit. Contrast with systems that commit all working changes directly. Some find the extra step confusing, and git commit -a stages tracked modifications automatically.`,
    f: ["What do index stages one, two, and three hold during a merge?","How does git add -p help build clean commits?"]
  },
  110: {
    a: `A remote-tracking branch, such as origin/main, is a local, read-only reference that records where a branch on a remote was at the time of your last communication with it. Stored under refs/remotes/origin/, it's updated by git fetch, git pull, and git push, never by commits you make locally, and it lets you see what the remote has without network access, and compare with your own work using commands like git log main..origin/main. A local branch is your own editable branch that moves when you commit. They are related through the upstream configuration: a local branch can be set to track a remote-tracking branch, recorded in .git/config as branch.<name>.remote and branch.<name>.merge. Tracking gives git status its 'ahead by 2, behind by 1' output, and allows git pull and git push without arguments to know where to go. Creating a local branch from a remote one, for example git switch feature when origin/feature exists, sets up tracking automatically; git push -u origin branch sets upstream on the first push, and push.autoSetupRemote true automates that. Key idea: origin/main is only your last known copy of the remote; to refresh, you fetch. Stale remote-tracking branches for branches deleted on the remote remain until you run git fetch --prune, or set fetch.prune true.`,
    f: ["What updates a remote-tracking branch?","What does git fetch --prune do?"]
  },
  111: {
    a: `These three mechanisms all stop Git from paying attention to files, but they work in different ways and have different intent. .gitignore is a versioned file, committed to the repository, listing patterns of untracked files that Git should ignore, such as build outputs, dependency folders, logs, and environment files. It's shared by the whole team, and nested .gitignore files apply to subdirectories. It only affects untracked files: a file already tracked continues to be tracked even if it matches a pattern, and must be removed from the index with git rm --cached. .git/info/exclude works the same as .gitignore but is local to your clone and not versioned or shared, ideal for personal files such as editor configurations, scratch notes, or local tooling that other developers don't have, and which don't belong in the project's shared ignore file. A global ignore file, set via core.excludesFile, serves personal patterns across all your repositories. assume-unchanged, git update-index --assume-unchanged file, is different: it tells Git to assume that a tracked file hasn't changed and skip checking it, as a performance hint for huge files. It is not a reliable way to ignore local modifications to tracked files, since Git may overwrite them during checkout or merge and silently lose your changes.`,
    f: ["Why does .gitignore not stop tracking an already tracked file?","What is skip-worktree for?"]
  },
  112: {
    a: `A branch convention should make purpose, ownership, and lifecycle obvious and be enforceable by tooling. Naming: use prefixes by type, such as feature/, fix/ or bugfix/, hotfix/, release/, chore/, and docs/, followed by a ticket ID and a short description in lowercase with hyphens, for example feature/PAY-1234-add-refund-endpoint. Including the ticket ID ties code to work tracking and enables automation, such as linking and status updates. Avoid personal names, uppercase and special characters, spaces, and overly long names. Long-lived branches should be few and protected: main, plus release/x.y for supported versions if needed. Lifecycle: branches are created from an up-to-date main, short-lived, ideally merged within a day or two, with small pull requests; merged branches are deleted automatically on merge using the platform setting; stale branches with no activity after a defined period, such as thirty days, are flagged by a bot and then removed, after notifying the owner. Rules: protected branches enforce reviews and checks; release branches are cut only when needed and receive only fixes via cherry-picks or backports; hotfixes branch from the production tag. Enforce naming with a branch name check in CI or push rulesets on the platform, which can reject non-conforming branch names. Document the policy in CONTRIBUTING. Use tags, not branches, for releases.`,
    f: ["Why include the ticket ID in the branch name?","How do you clean up stale branches?"]
  },
  113: {
    a: `Every Git commit records two identities with timestamps: the author and the committer. The author is the person who originally wrote the change; the committer is the person who applied it to the repository, that is, created this specific commit object. Often they are the same, but they differ when the commit is rewritten or applied by someone else: after git commit --amend or a rebase, the committer and committer date update to the person and time of the rewrite, while the author and author date stay as the original; when a maintainer applies a contributor's patch with git am, the contributor is the author and the maintainer the committer; cherry-picking preserves the author and sets you as the committer; and squash merges on platforms record the PR author as author, and the platform or merger as committer. Why it matters: credit and attribution stay with the original author through history rewrites; git log shows the author by default, with --pretty=fuller showing both; blame shows the author. For auditing and verification, note that both fields are just unauthenticated text set by configuration, user.name and user.email, or environment variables, so they can be forged; only signed commits give trustworthy identity.`,
    f: ["Which of the two changes after a rebase?","Why can author information be forged?"]
  },
  114: {
    a: `The terms overlap. A tracking branch, in the older terminology, is a local branch that is configured to track a remote branch: it has an upstream. The upstream branch is the remote branch that a local branch is linked to, recorded in config as branch.<name>.remote and branch.<name>.merge, and it determines the default targets for git pull, git push without arguments, and the ahead and behind counts in git status. The upstream is typically a remote-tracking ref such as origin/feature. Do not confuse these with remote-tracking branches, which are the local read-only copies like origin/feature that record the remote's state. Ways to set the upstream: when creating a local branch from a remote one, git switch feature or git checkout --track origin/feature sets it automatically; git branch --set-upstream-to=origin/feature sets it for the current branch (-u is a shortcut); git push -u origin feature pushes and sets upstream in one step; and push.autoSetupRemote true makes the first push set it up automatically. Inspect with git branch -vv, which shows each local branch, its upstream, and ahead and behind counts, and git rev-parse --abbrev-ref @{upstream}. Remove with git branch --unset-upstream. Also, push.default settings, with simple as the default, control what a plain git push does, usually pushing the current branch to the same-named upstream.`,
    f: ["How do you see each branch's upstream and ahead/behind counts?","What does push -u do?"]
  },
  115: {
    a: `git diff shows differences between states of the repository. It computes the changes between two trees or between a tree and the working directory, using a diff algorithm, by default Myers, to find a minimal set of line insertions and deletions, grouped into hunks with context lines. The forms to know: git diff compares the working tree with the index, the unstaged changes; git diff --staged compares the index with HEAD, what you are about to commit; git diff HEAD compares the working tree with the last commit; git diff A B compares two commits, branches, or tags; git diff A...B, three dots, compares B with the merge base of A and B, showing what B introduced, which is what a pull request shows; and git diff A..B is the same as two arguments. Useful options. --stat for a summary of files and line counts, --name-only and --name-status for lists of paths, -w or --ignore-all-space to ignore whitespace noise, --word-diff or --color-words for prose, --diff-algorithm=histogram or patience for more readable diffs on code with moves, -M and -C to detect renames and copies, --check to find whitespace errors, -U<n> to change context size, -S and -G to search for changes, and a path after -- to limit the scope.`,
    f: ["What is the difference between two-dot and three-dot diff?","Which option helps ignore whitespace noise?"]
  },
  116: {
    a: `Cleaning up branches safely means removing only those that are merged or confirmed abandoned, with a safety net. Local cleanup: first refresh and prune remote-tracking branches that no longer exist: git fetch --prune, or set fetch.prune true. List branches fully merged into main with git branch --merged main, excluding main and other protected names, then delete with git branch -d, which refuses to delete unmerged branches, unlike -D, which forces. A common one-liner pipes merged branches through grep to exclude protected ones into git branch -d. For squash-merged branches, git doesn't see them as merged, so use git branch -vv to find ones whose upstream is gone, and verify before force-deleting; helper tools like git-delete-squashed or checking against the PR state help. Remote cleanup at scale: enable the platform setting to automatically delete head branches after merge; use scripts or tooling via the API to find branches with no commits for a threshold such as ninety days and no open pull request, notify the owners and then delete after a grace period; skip protected and release branches by pattern.`,
    f: ["Why is git branch -d safer than -D?","How do you handle squash-merged branches?"]
  },
  117: {
    a: `When you run git commit, a series of objects are created. First, the index, the staging area, already contains the paths and blob hashes of the files to be committed. When you git add a file, Git hashes its content and writes a blob object, if one with that hash doesn't exist yet, and records it in the index. On commit, Git converts the index into tree objects: it builds a tree for each directory, bottom-up, with entries listing mode, name, and hash of blobs and subtrees; unchanged directories reuse existing tree objects, since identical content has identical hashes. The root tree's hash represents the whole snapshot. Next, Git creates a commit object containing the root tree hash, the parent commit hash, which is the current HEAD commit, or two for a merge, the author and committer identities with timestamps, and the commit message. This is hashed, giving the commit ID, and written to the object database as a loose object, zlib-compressed. Finally, Git updates the reference: the current branch file, such as refs/heads/main, is changed to the new commit's hash, and HEAD, which points at that branch, therefore points to the new commit; an entry is added to the reflog. Hooks run before and after: pre-commit, commit-msg, and post-commit.`,
    f: ["Why are unchanged directories not duplicated?","What does the commit object contain?"]
  },
  118: {
    a: `To keep a feature branch current with main, you can merge main into it or rebase it onto main. Merging main in creates a merge commit on the feature branch, preserving the actual history and never rewriting commits. It's safe for shared branches, since nobody's commits change, and conflicts are resolved once, in the merge commit. The downside is a noisy history with repeated merge commits from main inside the branch, which makes the branch's own changes harder to review and bisect, and the pull request's commit list can be cluttered. Rebasing replays the branch's commits on top of the latest main, creating new commits, so the history is linear and reads as if the work was done on current main. It produces a clean pull request and simplifies bisect and log. The downsides: it rewrites history, so commits get new hashes and a force push is needed; it's dangerous on branches shared with others, because their copies diverge, causing duplication and confusion; conflicts may need resolving repeatedly, once per replayed commit, though rerere helps; and it loses the record of when the integration happened.`,
    f: ["When is merging main into the feature branch preferable?","What does rebasing do to commit hashes?"]
  },
  119: {
    a: `Branches and tags are both references to commits, but with different semantics. A branch is a movable pointer: when you commit while on it, the pointer advances. It represents an ongoing line of development. A tag is intended to be a fixed label on a specific commit, marking a point in history, usually a release such as v1.4.0; it doesn't move when you commit. Tags live under refs/tags and, unlike branches, aren't normally checked out for work; checking one out gives a detached HEAD. Lightweight tags are simply a ref to a commit, like a branch that never moves, with no extra metadata. Annotated tags are full Git objects, created with git tag -a v1.4.0 -m 'message', that store the tagger's name, email, date, a message, and optionally a GPG or SSH signature, and they point to the commit. Annotated tags are the right choice for releases because they carry who, when, and why, can be signed and verified, and are shown in git describe by default. Lightweight tags suit temporary or private markers.`,
    f: ["Why use annotated tags for releases?","Are tags pushed automatically?"]
  },
  120: {
    a: `For a 50-engineer SaaS deploying many times a day, I'd choose trunk-based development with strong automation. Branching: main is the single long-lived branch, always deployable; engineers create short-lived topic branches, merged within a day, with pull requests of a few hundred lines; unfinished work is hidden behind feature flags; release branches only if a customer-facing version needs long-term support. Protection: rulesets on main requiring pull requests, one or two approvals, code owner review for sensitive paths, required status checks, a merge queue to keep main green, signed commits, no force pushes, linear history through squash merges with PR-title conventions. Quality gates: fast CI under ten minutes with caching and test sharding, linting, unit and integration tests, secret scanning with push protection, dependency and container scans, and pre-commit hooks for early feedback. Delivery: every merge to main builds an immutable artefact tagged with the commit SHA, deploys automatically to staging, runs smoke tests, then progressively to production using canary or blue-green with automated rollback; GitOps for configuration with environment folders; database migrations as backward-compatible, separate steps. Releases: semantic version tags generated from Conventional Commits, with automated changelogs.`,
    f: ["Why a merge queue at this team size and cadence?","How does a hotfix work in this model?"]
  },
  121: {
    a: `Git has three trees: the working directory, the index, and HEAD. The restore command was introduced to separate file-level operations from branch-level ones. Running restore on a path copies the version from the index into the working tree, discarding unstaged edits. Adding the staged option copies from HEAD into the index, which unstages the file without touching your edits. The source option lets me pull a file from any commit. The reset command, by contrast, mainly moves the current branch pointer, and its mixed default also resets the index. Checkout with double dash and a path does the same as restore but sits in a command that also switches branches, which caused many accidents. Discarding working tree changes is irreversible because they were never committed, so I check git diff first, or stash instead if I might want them back.`,
    f: ["Which of these can lose data permanently?","Why was restore introduced?"]
  },
  122: {
    a: `Git reads configuration from several files in order: system-wide, the user's global file, then the repository's own config, and optionally per-worktree. A narrower scope wins, so a local setting overrides a global one. I use local config for things that belong to a project, such as hooks path or line-ending rules, and global config for personal preferences like editor and aliases. A common production problem is committing with the wrong email, for example a personal address in a work repo. The includeIf directive solves this by loading an extra config file only when the repository lives under a given directory, so everything under my work folder automatically uses the work identity and signing key. When behaviour is unexpected, I run the list command with the show-origin flag, which prints the file and line that supplied every value, and that usually reveals the cause quickly.`,
    f: ["How do you fix commits with the wrong author?","What is a worktree config?"]
  },
  123: {
    a: `Status is more than a file list. The header tells me which branch I am on and how it relates to its upstream, for example ahead by two and behind by one, which means I have local commits to push and remote commits to integrate, and a diverged state needs a rebase or merge. It also reports when I am in the middle of a merge, rebase, cherry-pick or bisect, and lists the file categories separately: staged for the next commit, modified but unstaged, and untracked. The short form with the branch flag gives a compact view I use daily. For scripts and prompts I use the porcelain format, which is designed to remain stable, rather than parsing human text. To see divergence numerically, I use rev-list with left-right and count between the upstream and HEAD. Reading these signals before pushing prevents most rejected pushes and surprise merges.`,
    f: ["What does diverged mean?","Why use porcelain output?"]
  },
  124: {
    a: `When I have been working for a while, my working tree often contains a bug fix, a refactor and a debugging print mixed together. Committing everything at once makes review and later bisecting harder. Patch mode walks through each hunk of change and asks whether to stage it. I answer yes or no, use the split option when a hunk contains two unrelated edits, and the edit option when I need to hand-trim a hunk. The result is a series of small commits that each do one thing and pass tests. After staging, I inspect the staged diff to verify it contains only what I intend, and run tests before committing. The same interactive approach exists for restore and stash, which lets me discard or stash only part of my changes. Clean commits make code review faster, make revert safe, and make bisect point to a precise cause.`,
    f: ["How do you split a hunk further?","Why do small commits help bisect?"]
  },
  125: {
    a: `The merge base is the most recent commit that two branches share, and it is the starting point for a three-way merge and for judging what each branch changed. Range syntax builds on it. Double dot in log means commits reachable from the right side that are not reachable from the left, so main dot dot feature lists what the feature adds. Triple dot in log shows the symmetric difference: commits on either side but not both, and with the left-right flag it marks which side each came from, which is useful to see divergence. In diff the meaning changes: triple dot compares the right side against the merge base, which shows only what the branch introduced, even if main has moved on. That is exactly what a reviewer wants for a pull request. Mixing up the two forms is a classic source of confusing output.`,
    f: ["Why does diff triple-dot differ from log?","What is a symmetric difference?"]
  },
  126: {
    a: `Creating a branch is purely local: switch with the create flag makes a new branch at the current commit and moves HEAD to it. The remote does not know about it until I push. A tracking relationship stores which remote branch is the counterpart, so status can show ahead and behind counts, and pull and push work without arguments. I normally establish it with push and the set-upstream flag the first time I publish, which pushes and records the link in one step. If the branch already exists on the remote, I can attach it afterwards with the set-upstream-to option on the branch command. To avoid forgetting, I enable the autoSetupRemote setting so the first push sets tracking automatically. Naming matters as well: a convention with a type prefix and ticket ID makes branches easy to filter and automate.`,
    f: ["What does push.default control?","How do you see tracking info for all branches?"]
  },
  127: {
    a: `Teams that support several versions at once keep a long-lived release branch per supported line, such as release 2.4 and release 2.3. A bug fix is first made on the main branch, so future releases include it, then it is backported to each supported branch. I cherry-pick the fix commit using the x flag, which appends the original commit hash to the message, creating traceability. Conflicts are possible because the older branch has diverged, so I resolve them and rerun the tests for that version rather than assuming the same fix works. After merge I tag a patch release with an annotated tag and publish artifacts. To scale this, many teams use bot automation where a label on the pull request opens backport pull requests automatically. I use branch contains to verify which branches already include a fix, and I retire release branches according to a published support policy.`,
    f: ["Why fix main first?","What does cherry-pick -x add?"]
  },
  128: {
    a: `Long-lived branches exist because a feature cannot ship until it is finished, so developers isolate it, and the longer it lives, the worse the merge pain and integration surprises become. Feature flags break that coupling. I merge small increments to the main branch continuously, with the unfinished code hidden behind a runtime switch that defaults to off. Deployment then no longer equals release: the code is in production but dormant, and I enable it for internal users, then a percentage of traffic, and can turn it off instantly without a redeploy if metrics worsen. This supports trunk-based development and quick reviews of small changes. The costs are extra code paths to test, the need for flag management tooling, and the risk of stale flags, so each flag gets an owner and a removal date and is cleaned up soon after full rollout.`,
    f: ["What are the risks of flags?","How do you test both flag states?"]
  },
  129: {
    a: `The rename itself is trivial, but the references to the old name are everywhere. My plan starts with an inventory: CI pipeline triggers, deployment scripts, branch protection rules, merge queue settings, code owners, badges, documentation and any automation that hard-codes the name. Then I rename on the hosting platform, which usually retargets open pull requests and offers redirects for web links. I update the protection rules and pipeline conditions at the same time, ideally using a window with low activity. Each developer then updates their clone by renaming the local branch, fetching, setting the new upstream and refreshing the remote HEAD pointer. I send a short note with the exact commands. Afterwards I watch for pipelines that silently stopped triggering, and keep the old branch name as a protected alias for a short period if integrations are slow to update.`,
    f: ["What breaks silently after a rename?","How do forks get updated?"]
  },
  130: {
    a: `A big feature is hard to review as one pull request, so I split it into a chain where branch two is based on branch one, and branch three on branch two, each a small reviewable change that targets its predecessor. Reviewers can approve each piece independently, and merging proceeds from the bottom up. The difficulty arises when a lower branch changes after review feedback, because the branches above it must be rebased on the new base. Newer Git versions help with the update-refs option, which moves every branch in the stack during a single rebase, and there are tools that automate restacking and opening pull requests. I always push with force-with-lease because rebased branches need force. Stacking is worth it for large, naturally sequential work, but for small changes it adds overhead, so I prefer short independent pull requests when possible.`,
    f: ["What is force-with-lease?","When is stacking not worth it?"]
  },
  131: {
    a: `A merge strategy is the algorithm that combines trees. Modern Git uses ort by default, which is fast and handles renames well. Strategy options adjust behaviour: the X theirs and X ours options resolve only the conflicting hunks in favour of one side while still merging the non-conflicting changes from both, which is sometimes useful for generated files. That is very different from the ours strategy, which creates a merge commit that keeps my tree exactly as it was and just records the other branch as merged, which I use to mark an obsolete branch as integrated without taking its content. The subtree strategy merges into a subdirectory for vendored projects. The danger is that automatic preference hides real conflicts and silently drops someone's work, so I use these sparingly, review the result with diff, and prefer manual resolution for source code.`,
    f: ["What is the difference between -X ours and -s ours?","Why is patience diff useful?"]
  },
  132: {
    a: `By default, conflict markers show only my side and theirs, which forces guessing about intent. Setting the conflict style to diff3, or the cleaner zdiff3, adds a third section with the original common ancestor, so I can see what each side changed relative to the base and combine both intents correctly. For bigger conflicts I configure a graphical or terminal mergetool that opens base, local, remote and the merged result in a four-pane view. Git also exposes the three versions as stages in the index, which I can inspect with the checkout options for ours and theirs, or the diff against base. After resolving I search for leftover markers, run the build and tests, and only then add and continue. With rerere enabled, Git remembers my resolutions so repeated rebases do not require resolving the same conflict again.`,
    f: ["What does zdiff3 improve?","What are index stages 1, 2 and 3?"]
  },
  133: {
    a: `A squash merge creates one new commit on main containing the content of the whole branch, but it does not record the branch's commits as ancestors. If another branch was built on top of that original branch, Git later thinks the original commits are still unmerged, and a regular rebase or merge replays them, producing conflicts with changes that main already contains. The fix is to tell Git exactly which commits to carry over. I use rebase with the onto option, giving the new base as main, the old branch tip as the upstream to exclude, and the child branch to move. This replays only the child's own commits. The cherry command helps verify what is considered new. To avoid the problem, I rebase child branches immediately after the parent is squashed, or avoid squash merging when stacked work is expected.`,
    f: ["Why does squash lose ancestry?","What does git cherry show?"]
  },
  134: {
    a: `If a merge stops with conflicts and I decide it is not the right time, the abort option returns the working tree and index to how they were before the merge started, as long as I did not have uncommitted changes that got mixed in, which is why I start merges from a clean tree. The reset with the merge option is the underlying mechanism and works when abort refuses. If I already completed the merge commit locally but have not pushed, I can move the branch back using ORIG_HEAD, which Git sets to the previous tip before risky operations. If the merge is already pushed and others may have pulled it, I never rewrite history; I revert the merge commit and specify the mainline parent, which makes a new commit undoing the changes. Note that reverting a merge affects later re-merging of that branch, which I handle by reverting the revert.`,
    f: ["What is ORIG_HEAD?","Why does reverting a merge need -m?"]
  },
  135: {
    a: `During code review I often need to change something that belongs in an earlier commit of my branch. Rather than amending blindly, I commit the correction with the fixup option pointing at the target commit, which creates a commit whose message starts with fixup and the original title. Reviewers can read the small incremental commit, which makes re-review easy. When the review is finished, I run an interactive rebase against the base branch with the autosquash option, and Git automatically reorders each fixup right after its target and marks it to be squashed in, so I only need to save the todo list. The result is a clean, logical history. Because the commits are rewritten, I push with force-with-lease, and only do this on my own feature branch, never on a shared branch that others are building on.`,
    f: ["What is the difference between fixup and squash?","Why force-with-lease and not force?"]
  },
  136: {
    a: `When I need to know when a constant or function appeared, disappeared or changed, searching the current file is not enough. The S option, known as the pickaxe, lists commits where the count of a given string changed, so it finds the commit that introduced or deleted it. The G option takes a regular expression and finds commits whose diff lines match, which also catches modifications. Adding the patch flag shows the actual change, and restricting to a path speeds it up. For tracking a function across refactors, the L option follows a function name or line range through history. If a file was renamed, the follow option keeps tracing before the rename. Together these answer why a line exists and who changed it, which is essential for incident investigation and for understanding a legacy decision before changing it.`,
    f: ["Difference between -S and -G?","What does -L do?"]
  },
  137: {
    a: `Sometimes a repository needs a global history change: remove a leaked credential file, strip a huge binary, correct author emails, or split a directory out into its own repository. The filter-repo tool does this quickly and safely, and replaced the old filter-branch command, which was slow and error-prone. I run it in a fresh mirror clone, specify the path to remove or the text to replace, and it rewrites all commits and refs. The consequences are significant: every affected commit gets a new hash, so existing clones, forks, open pull requests, tags and signatures no longer match. I coordinate a freeze, force-push the rewritten refs, ask everyone to reclone rather than merge, and ask the hosting provider to purge cached objects. For leaked secrets, rewriting history is secondary to rotating the credential, because copies may already exist elsewhere.`,
    f: ["Why must everyone reclone?","Why is rotation more important for leaks?"]
  },
  138: {
    a: `Blame annotates each line with the last commit that changed it, which is a starting point, not an answer. A formatting run or a rename across the repository makes every line point at one uninteresting commit. To see through that, I use the whitespace flag, and the move and copy detection flags, which attribute lines to their original commit even if they were moved between files. For team-wide cleanliness, I keep a file listing the hashes of bulk formatting commits and configure blame to ignore them, and hosting platforms read the same file. Once I find the commit, I read its message and linked pull request to understand the reasoning, then use pickaxe search or line history if the code has been reworked several times. Blame should drive better questions, never blame people, so I frame investigations around the change, not the author.`,
    f: ["What does -C detect?","How do ignore-revs files work?"]
  },
  139: {
    a: `A reliable changelog starts with consistent input. I adopt a convention such as conventional commits, where each message begins with a type like feat, fix or chore, and an optional scope, and I enforce it with a commit-msg hook and a CI check on pull request titles. Then notes can be generated from the range between the previous tag and the new one, grouped by type, with breaking changes called out from a marker in the message. I find the previous tag with describe, list non-merge commits in the range, and render them via a release tool or a simple script. For squash-merge workflows, the pull request title becomes the commit message, so I enforce title format instead. A human still reviews the generated text to remove noise and add context for users, and the release notes link to issues and upgrade guidance where needed.`,
    f: ["What are conventional commits?","How do squash merges affect changelogs?"]
  },
  140: {
    a: `A frequent operational question is whether a particular fix is included in the version running in production. If I have the commit hash, I ask Git which tags contain it, and which remote branches contain it; no output for the release tag means it is not included. To find what is waiting for the next release, I list commits reachable from main but not from the last release tag, optionally limited to a path or a team's directory. When release branches get cherry-picked fixes, the hashes differ, so the cherry command compares patch contents and marks which commits are already applied. For traceability, I include the original hash in backport messages and use annotated tags on each release. These queries replace guessing and spreadsheets, and they are quick to run before a release or during an incident.`,
    f: ["How to check what is in the deployed build?","What does git cherry compare?"]
  },
  141: {
    a: `I want review to be fast, meaningful and enforced by the system. Authors open a draft pull request early to get CI running and feedback on direction, then mark it ready when complete. Required status checks, such as build, tests, linting and security scans, must pass before merge. A CODEOWNERS file maps directories to owning teams so the right reviewers are requested automatically, and branch protection requires at least one approval, dismisses stale approvals when new commits arrive, and blocks direct pushes to the main branch. To keep reviews quick, I encourage small, focused changes with a clear description of what and why, link the ticket, and include test evidence. I track metrics such as time to first review and pull request size, because chronic delays usually indicate large changes or unclear ownership. Auto-merge after approval removes idle waiting.`,
    f: ["Why dismiss stale approvals?","How do you keep PRs small?"]
  },
  142: {
    a: `In the forking workflow each contributor has their own server-side copy of the repository. They clone their fork, add the original project as a second remote usually named upstream, create a branch for their change, push it to their fork, and open a pull request against the upstream main branch. Maintainers need to grant write access only to a small group, which is why open source projects and cross-company collaborations use it. The contributor must keep their fork current by fetching upstream and rebasing or merging, otherwise their pull request conflicts or lacks recent changes. Inside a single company with trusted teams, the shared repository with short-lived branches is simpler, avoids duplicate storage and fork drift, and lets CI use repository secrets safely. Pull requests from forks usually run CI with reduced permissions for security reasons.`,
    f: ["Why do fork PRs get limited secrets?","How do you sync a fork?"]
  },
  143: {
    a: `The idea is that the history itself drives versioning. Developers write commit messages that follow a convention: feat for a new feature, fix for a bug fix, and an exclamation mark or a breaking change footer for incompatible changes. A CI job on the main branch analyses the commits since the last release tag and computes the next semantic version: a major bump for breaking changes, minor for features, patch for fixes. It then creates the tag, builds artifacts, publishes the package and writes the changelog automatically. To keep it trustworthy, I validate commit messages or pull request titles in CI, require squash merging with a well-formed title, and use protected tag rules so only the release pipeline identity can create version tags. A dry run on pull requests shows maintainers the version impact before merging.`,
    f: ["What triggers a major bump?","Why protect tags?"]
  },
  144: {
    a: `Freezing the main branch stops all progress and creates a rush afterwards, so I prefer a release train model. At a scheduled time or when criteria are met, I cut a release branch from a known good commit on main. Main stays open for new work, and the release branch accepts only approved fixes, cherry-picked from main with traceability, with a stricter protection rule and the full test suite required. The release is tagged and deployed from that branch. If a real freeze is needed, for example during a holiday, I enforce it with branch rules or a status check that fails unless the change has an exception label, rather than relying on emails. A merge queue keeps the main branch green by testing each change on top of the latest queue before merging. Clear communication of dates, exceptions and an on-call approver prevent confusion.`,
    f: ["Why a release branch over freezing main?","What does a merge queue test?"]
  },
  145: {
    a: `Asynchronous work fails when pull requests need conversation to be understood. So I write descriptions that say what changed, why, how it was tested, and what to look at first, with screenshots or logs where helpful. Automated checks provide instant feedback so authors do not wait for humans to find trivial issues. Pull requests stay small, so a reviewer can finish in one sitting, and I use CODEOWNERS plus a rota so each change has a named reviewer who covers the other time zone. Branch conventions and the rule of integrating often reduce conflicts when people hand work over. Settings like automatic pruning on fetch and a consistent pull strategy keep local clones tidy. Decisions are recorded in the pull request or in short architecture decision records, so the next person understands the reasoning without asking.`,
    f: ["How do you reduce review latency across zones?","What belongs in a PR description?"]
  },
  146: {
    a: `Git rarely deletes objects immediately. After a hard reset, a bad rebase or dropping a stash, the commits still exist in the object database, just unreferenced. First I check the reflog, which records where HEAD and each branch pointed over time; I find the entry from before the mistake and create a branch there. If the reflog does not help, for example because the commit was never checked out, I run fsck with the lost-found option, which lists dangling commits and blobs, and I inspect candidates with show to identify the right one. A dropped stash is a dangling commit, and the fsck output shows its hash so I can apply it. As soon as I find the work, I create a branch or tag pointing at it so garbage collection cannot remove it. The longer I wait, the greater the risk after automatic pruning.`,
    f: ["How long do unreachable objects live?","How do you recover a dropped stash?"]
  },
  147: {
    a: `First I stop and tell the team, so nobody pulls or deploys the bad state, and check whether a pipeline already started. Then I save the work: create a new branch at the commit so the changes are not lost, and I can open a proper pull request from it. To remove the mistake from the shared branch, the safest approach is to revert the commits, which adds new commits that undo them and is safe for everyone who has already fetched. If the push was seconds ago, protection rules allow it and nobody has pulled, I might reset the branch to the previous good commit and force push with a lease, but I treat that as exceptional and coordinate it. Afterwards, I fix the root cause, usually missing branch protection, and write a brief review so the guardrail exists.`,
    f: ["Why is revert safer than reset here?","What guardrail prevents this?"]
  },
  148: {
    a: `This message means that Git cannot find a merge base between my branch and the remote branch. Typical causes are a repository that was re-initialised and force-pushed, a history rewrite such as a filter-repo run, or a local repo created independently and later given the remote. Before overriding the warning, I find out which it is. If the remote was rewritten, the right fix is to rebase my local commits onto the new history using the onto option, naming the old base commit, or simply create a new branch from the remote and cherry-pick my work. If I intentionally want to combine two independent repositories, the allow-unrelated-histories option performs the merge and I resolve the resulting conflicts. Blindly merging after a rewrite brings old and new copies of the same commits together and creates a confusing, duplicated history.`,
    f: ["What causes unrelated histories?","How do you carry commits after a rewrite?"]
  },
  149: {
    a: `A rebase rewrites my commits, but the old ones remain in the object store, and the reflog remembers where the branch was before. If the rebase is still in progress and going badly, I abort it. If it completed and I realise the result is wrong, I look at the reflog, find the entry that says rebase started or the position just before, and reset the branch hard to that entry. Git also sets ORIG_HEAD to the previous tip when running a rebase, so resetting to it often works immediately, but another operation such as a merge or reset can overwrite it, so the reflog is the dependable source. If I had already force-pushed the rebased branch, restoring requires pushing the old tip again with a lease, and teammates who pulled the new version need to be told. This safety net is why rewriting local history is low risk.`,
    f: ["Is ORIG_HEAD always reliable?","What if you already force-pushed?"]
  },
  150: {
    a: `Fsck checks the integrity of the object database and reports corruption, missing objects and dangling commits. It is read-only and safe. Prune permanently removes unreachable objects, and garbage collection repacks objects for efficiency and, by default, prunes unreachable ones older than a grace period. These are exactly the tools that can destroy my chance of recovering lost work. So the first step in any recovery is to copy the entire repository directory as a backup, and work on the copy. I avoid running gc, prune or reflog expire until I have found and branched the commits I need, and I can even set the prune expiry to never to be safe. Automatic gc can trigger after some commands, so for a precious situation I temporarily disable it in the config. Once everything is saved and verified, normal maintenance can resume.`,
    f: ["Why copy .git first?","What does auto gc prune?"]
  },
  151: {
    a: `I begin with the description and linked ticket to understand the intent, then check whether the approach fits the design before reading details. Next I review correctness: edge cases, error handling, concurrency and data migrations. I check that tests cover the behaviour, including failure paths, and I look for security issues like input validation, secrets and permissions, plus performance concerns such as queries in loops. For operability, I ask about logging, metrics, rollout and rollback. For risky changes I check the branch out and run it. Style issues should be handled by linters, not humans. My comments are specific and actionable, distinguish must-fix from suggestions, and explain the why, in a respectful tone. I acknowledge good work too. Large pull requests I ask to split, because review quality drops sharply with size. When done I approve clearly or request changes with reasons.`,
    f: ["What should linters handle instead of humans?","How do you review a very large PR?"]
  },
  152: {
    a: `When an author rebases and force-pushes a pull request after feedback, the hosting UI often shows the whole diff again, and reviewers cannot tell which parts changed in response to comments. Range-diff solves this by matching the commits of the old series to the new series and showing the differences between corresponding commits, with markers for unchanged, modified, added or dropped commits. As a reviewer, I keep the previous tip, which is available in my reflog or from the remote-tracking branch before fetching, and run range-diff with the common base and both ranges. As an author, I help reviewers by mentioning what changed in each revision, or by adding fixup commits during review and squashing only at the end. Some platforms now offer a compare-force-push view with similar value. It makes iterative review of rebased series practical.`,
    f: ["What does range-diff output mean?","How can authors make re-review easier?"]
  },
  153: {
    a: `After rewriting my own branch with rebase or amend, a normal push is rejected because histories diverged, so I need to force. The plain force option replaces the remote branch no matter what, so if a colleague pushed a commit to it meanwhile, it is destroyed. The lease variant adds a condition: the push only succeeds if the remote branch is still at the commit I last saw, so it fails if someone else has pushed, and I can fetch and review first. A subtlety is that an automatic background fetch can update my remote-tracking reference without me inspecting the changes, which defeats the safety, and the force-if-includes option guards against that. For shared branches such as main and release branches, I rely on server-side protection to forbid force pushes entirely, and the rule of rewriting only branches that I own.`,
    f: ["When does lease protect you?","Which branches should forbid force-push?"]
  },
  154: {
    a: `A repository can have any number of named remotes. In a typical case, origin is my fork or the team repository and upstream is the original project, or a vendor remote carries a third-party codebase I patch. I add each with a name, fetch them individually or all together with pruning, and use remote-tracking branches such as vendor main to compare and merge. For redundancy, a remote can have multiple push URLs so a single push updates the primary and a backup mirror. The mirror option pushes all refs and deletes anything missing locally, which is powerful for creating exact copies but dangerous if pointed at the wrong place, so it is reserved for dedicated mirrors. I keep naming consistent across the team, check the configured remotes with the verbose list, and make upstream pushes impossible by using read-only credentials.`,
    f: ["What does --mirror do?","How do you merge vendor updates?"]
  },
  155: {
    a: `Relying on each developer's autocrlf setting leads to inconsistent line endings and noisy diffs where every line appears changed. A better approach is to store the rules in the repository with a gitattributes file, so everyone gets the same behaviour. I set text to auto for general files, which lets Git detect text and normalise to LF in the repository, and add explicit rules where the working-tree ending matters: LF for shell scripts and Makefiles, CRLF for Windows batch files, and a binary marker for images and archives. When introducing the file into an existing repository, I run a renormalise once and commit the result in a separate, clearly labelled commit, and add it to the blame ignore file so history stays useful. An editorconfig file complements this by guiding editors, and a CI check can catch accidental CRLF in scripts.`,
    f: ["Why pin scripts to LF?","How do you renormalise an existing repo?"]
  },
  156: {
    a: `Cloning a huge repository with full history on every CI job wastes minutes and bandwidth. I reduce the cost in layers. A shallow clone with a small depth fetches only recent commits, which is enough for most build and test jobs. A partial clone with the blob filter downloads commit and tree objects up front but fetches file contents lazily, so only what the job needs is transferred. Combined with sparse checkout, a job in a monorepo materialises only its own directories. For self-hosted runners, a persistent reference mirror or cache on the runner means a clone borrows objects locally. The trade-off is that some jobs need history, such as those that compute versions from tags, run blame-based analysis, or diff against the merge base, so I configure those with a deeper fetch or unshallow explicitly. Measuring checkout time per job shows where to focus.`,
    f: ["What does the blob:none filter do?","Which jobs need full history?"]
  },
  157: {
    a: `Running every test for every change does not scale in a monorepo. I find the changed files by diffing the pull request head against the merge base with the target branch, then map those files to projects. Simple path filters in the CI configuration work for independent services, but they miss indirect effects, so I prefer a build tool that understands the dependency graph and can list affected projects, meaning that a change in a shared library triggers tests for all its consumers. Changes to global configuration, the lockfile or the pipeline itself trigger the full suite. The main branch still runs everything, perhaps nightly or on merge, as a safety net, and a merge queue validates the combined result. Remote caching of build and test results avoids rerunning unchanged work. I monitor flaky tests and skipped-test accuracy, since a wrong dependency map silently lets bugs through.`,
    f: ["Why isn't path filtering enough?","Why still run everything on main?"]
  },
  158: {
    a: `Anyone who can create a tag or merge to the release branch can trigger production changes, so those actions are guarded. Tag creation is limited by protected tag rules to the release team or the pipeline. The workflow triggered by a tag deploys to production only through a protected environment that requires manual approval from designated reviewers and optionally a wait timer. The job obtains short-lived cloud credentials by OIDC, scoped to the repository and environment, so no long-lived secrets are stored. It deploys an immutable artifact that was built and tested earlier, identified by digest, rather than rebuilding from source, which guarantees that what was tested is what ships. Signed tags let the job verify the release, and every deployment records the commit, the approver and the artifact version for audit. Rollback is redeploying a previous artifact.`,
    f: ["Why build once and promote?","What does an environment protection rule do?"]
  },
  159: {
    a: `Flaky tests erode trust: people re-run until green, real failures get ignored, and merges slow down. I treat flakiness as a defect with an owner. First, measure: collect pass-fail history per test so I know which are unreliable, and detect them by re-running suspicious tests many times in isolation or in random order. Next, move confirmed flaky tests to a quarantine suite that still runs and reports but does not block merges, with a ticket, an owner and a deadline. A limited automatic retry may be used for known infrastructure noise, but retries are logged and visible so they are not hiding problems. Then fix the root causes, which are commonly timing assumptions, shared state, order dependence, network calls and unmocked time. The required checks stay strict for healthy tests, so the gate keeps its meaning, and a dashboard shows the flake rate trending down.`,
    f: ["How do you detect flakiness?","Why is quarantine with deadlines important?"]
  },
  160: {
    a: `Signing proves that a commit really came from the holder of a key, not just someone who typed an author name. Modern Git supports signing with SSH keys, which is easier than GPG since developers already have them. I configure the signing format, point the signing key at the public key, and enable signing by default for commits and tags. The hosting platform needs the same public key registered as a signing key so it can display a verified badge. For enforcement, branch rules can require signed commits so unsigned work cannot be merged. In CI, I can verify signatures against an allowed signers file that maps identities to public keys, failing the build if a commit is unsigned or signed by an unknown key. Key rotation and revocation need a process, and hardware-backed keys add protection against theft.`,
    f: ["Why choose SSH over GPG?","How do you revoke a signing key?"]
  },
  161: {
    a: `Secrets in Git are dangerous because history is permanent and widely copied. I use scanners that examine every commit across all branches with entropy checks and patterns for known providers, ideally with verification against the provider so findings are real. A pre-commit hook catches mistakes on the developer machine before they enter history, a CI job scans pull requests as a backstop because hooks can be skipped, and the hosting platform's push protection blocks pushes containing known secret formats. For existing repositories, I run a full-history scan once, triage the results, and treat each live secret as compromised: rotate it first, then optionally rewrite history to remove it. I maintain an allowlist for false positives with reasons. Education matters too, so developers use environment variables, secret managers and injected credentials rather than committing config files.`,
    f: ["Why isn't a pre-commit hook enough?","What do you do with a found secret?"]
  },
  162: {
    a: `The goal is that the right identity gets the least access for the shortest time. Humans sign in through the company identity provider with SSO and multi-factor authentication, so offboarding removes access everywhere. For SSH, I prefer short-lived certificates issued by a certificate authority after authentication, instead of long-lived keys that sit on laptops forever. Automation uses scoped credentials: deploy keys limited to one repository and read-only by default, or app tokens and OIDC-issued tokens limited to specific repositories and permissions that expire within minutes. Personal access tokens are discouraged for CI because they inherit a person's broad rights and outlive their role. I organise access by teams with roles rather than individuals, review membership and token inventory periodically, and send audit logs to the SIEM to alert on unusual clone volumes or permission changes.`,
    f: ["Why avoid PATs in CI?","How do SSH certificates help offboarding?"]
  },
  163: {
    a: `A repository can contain content that executes when I run normal commands. Hooks themselves are not transferred by clone, but build scripts, test runners and package manifests obviously run code. Git-specific vectors include smudge and clean filters or external diff drivers referenced by attributes, which can run commands if the matching configuration exists, submodule URLs that use unexpected protocols or point at local paths, and past vulnerabilities in handling of symlinks and case-insensitive paths. To reduce exposure, I keep Git updated, restrict allowed transport protocols such as ext and local file, inspect the submodule file before updating submodules, and clone with checkout disabled to examine contents first. Reviewing unknown code happens in a disposable container or virtual machine with no credentials. Automated tooling that clones user-supplied repositories, like CI for forks, must run with minimal privileges and no secrets.`,
    f: ["Are hooks cloned?","Why sandbox untrusted repos?"]
  },
  164: {
    a: `Compliance frameworks ask that no single person can push unreviewed code to production, that changes are traceable, and that records cannot be altered. I implement separation of duties with branch protection requiring approval from a reviewer who is not the author, passing required checks, and no bypass for administrators except via a logged break-glass process. Signed commits and tags support integrity and identity. Every change links to a ticket through branch or commit conventions, and the first-parent history of main shows each merged pull request with its approvals. The hosting platform's audit log, covering permission changes, protection edits and token usage, is exported to long-term storage per retention rules. Releases are tagged and associated with deployment records. At audit time I can sample a commit and show the ticket, review, test results and deployment, which provides the evidence trail.`,
    f: ["How do you handle break-glass access?","What evidence does an auditor sample?"]
  },
  165: {
    a: `A shallow clone limits how many commits of history are downloaded, so operations like log, blame and merge-base may not work properly beyond the cut. A partial clone keeps the complete commit history but uses a filter to skip some objects at clone time. For example, the blob none filter downloads commits and trees but not file contents, and Git fetches missing blobs on demand from the promisor remote, which is the server that promises to supply them later. This gives a fast initial clone with full history for log and graph operations, and file contents arrive when I check out or diff. The trade-off is network dependency during later operations, so offline work can fail on missing objects. Tree-less clones go further. I choose blobless for developers and shallow for ephemeral CI.`,
    f: ["What is a promisor remote?","What breaks offline in a partial clone?"]
  },
  166: {
    a: `As a repository grows, loose objects, many small pack files and stale metadata slow down status, log and fetch. Garbage collection can fix this, but running it automatically in the foreground interrupts work. The maintenance command schedules smaller background jobs using the operating system scheduler: hourly prefetch of remote changes, commit-graph updates that accelerate history traversal, incremental repacking that consolidates packs without a heavy full rewrite, and loose-object packing. I enable it per repository with the start command, which registers the repo and installs the schedule, and can choose the incremental strategy. Combined with the filesystem monitor and untracked cache for status speed, it keeps large monorepos responsive. I verify results with object statistics and timing common commands before and after, and keep an occasional full gc on the server side.`,
    f: ["What does prefetch do?","What is the commit-graph?"]
  },
  167: {
    a: `By default status must examine every tracked file's metadata and scan directories for untracked files, which on a repository with hundreds of thousands of files takes seconds. The built-in file system monitor registers with the operating system to be told which paths changed since the last query, so Git only inspects those. The untracked cache stores the modification times of directories, so it can skip rescanning directories that have not changed. Enabling them is a configuration change, and they work best together with periodic maintenance. The caveats are network file systems and some container mounts where change notifications are unreliable, and rare cases where a stale cache needs to be reset. I measure with performance tracing before and after to confirm the benefit, and I recommend them in the onboarding guide for monorepo developers.`,
    f: ["Where does fsmonitor not work well?","How do you measure the speedup?"]
  },
  168: {
    a: `Commit messages are immutable once shared, because changing them changes the hash. But sometimes I want to attach information after the fact, for example the deployment time, test run link or compliance approval. Git notes store annotations in a separate ref, keyed by the commit hash, so the commit itself stays unchanged. I can view them in log output and edit them as needed. The catch is that standard push and fetch do not transfer notes, so I either push and fetch the notes ref explicitly or add the refspec to the remote configuration. Many hosting platforms do not display notes, which limits adoption, and rewriting commits through rebase orphans their notes unless the rewrite is configured to copy them. For those reasons, many teams prefer tags, release records or external systems, but notes remain useful in tooling and specialised workflows.`,
    f: ["Why are notes not shared by default?","What happens to notes after a rebase?"]
  },
  169: {
    a: `In a monorepo, an engineer rarely needs every directory. Sparse checkout lets me materialise only selected folders in the working tree, while the repository still contains everything. The cone mode restricts patterns to whole directories, which allows fast matching, and it always includes files at the repository root. The index is a file listing every tracked path, so with a million files even a sparse working tree makes index operations slow. The sparse index feature collapses excluded directories into single placeholder entries, so the index scales with what I use, not with the whole repository, which speeds up status, add and checkout. Combined with a partial clone, I get small downloads and a small working tree. Tools that expand the index, like some IDE integrations or full-tree commands, may undo the benefit, so I check compatibility and document the recommended setup.`,
    f: ["What is cone mode?","What can expand a sparse index?"]
  },
  170: {
    a: `I begin with discovery: repository size, history length, binary assets, branch and tag layout, users and integrations such as CI and issue links. Then I decide how much history to keep, since full conversion of decades of history may be unnecessary, while archiving the old system read-only. I create an authors mapping from usernames to names and emails. A conversion tool such as git svn or a dedicated converter produces a Git repository, followed by cleanup: convert svn branches and tags into Git refs, move large binaries to LFS, remove junk, and possibly split into multiple repositories if the original was a monolith of unrelated projects. I validate by comparing file trees at selected revisions with the source. A trial migration rehearses timing. Cutover includes a freeze, final sync, updating CI, hooks and permissions, and training with cheat sheets, followed by support during the first weeks.`,
    f: ["How much history do you convert?","How do you validate the conversion?"]
  },
  171: {
    a: `A change can pass CI on its branch yet break main when combined with another change merged in the meantime, a semantic conflict that Git does not detect as a textual one. My first priority is to get main green again for everyone: identify the merge that introduced the failure by looking at the first-parent history and the last green build, and revert it if the fix is not obvious and quick, since a fast revert unblocks the team. Then I investigate calmly, using bisect with an automated test script when several merges are candidates, to find the exact interaction, and prepare a corrected change. To prevent recurrence, I enable a merge queue, which tests each pull request on top of the latest main plus the queued changes before merging, or require branches to be up to date before merging. I also record the incident and improve the tests that failed to catch it.`,
    f: ["Why revert before investigating?","How does a merge queue prevent this?"]
  },
  172: {
    a: `The immediate concern is security, not the lost commits. I ask the owner to report it, then revoke the SSH keys and personal tokens registered to their account, end active sessions, and rotate any secrets that were stored on the device, such as cloud credentials and environment files. I review the audit log for unusual activity from their account after the loss, and confirm whether disk encryption and a lock screen were enabled, which determines how likely it is that data can be read. Then I deal with lost work: check whether any of it exists on remote branches, in the CI system or in editor backups, and help the developer reconstruct the rest. Going forward I encourage pushing work-in-progress to personal remote branches daily, enforce full-disk encryption through device management, use short-lived credentials, and avoid keeping secrets in local files.`,
    f: ["What gets revoked first?","How do you reduce the impact of lost devices?"]
  },
  173: {
    a: `Repeated conflicts and overwrites show that a file serves too many owners. I start with data: how many commits touched it recently, who changed it, and what kinds of edits. Then I address the structure. If different teams manage different sections, I split the file into per-team or per-service files that are combined at build or deploy time, so edits rarely collide. If it is generated information, I generate it from a single source of truth. CODEOWNERS makes ownership explicit, requiring each team to approve changes to its own part. Process changes help too: shorter-lived branches, rebasing before merge, and a merge queue, so conflicts surface early in CI rather than after merge. If overwrites came from careless conflict resolution, I add review for the config and automated validation, such as a schema check, that fails when keys disappear unexpectedly.`,
    f: ["Why is this a design smell?","How would a schema check help?"]
  },
  174: {
    a: `Once an artifact built from a tag is published and consumers may have pulled it, the tag becomes part of the record. Deleting and re-pushing it to a different commit means the same version number would refer to two different contents, breaking checksums, caches and trust. So I compare what the tag points to with what should have been released, decide the impact, and release a new corrected version number from the right commit. The wrong version is marked as deprecated or yanked in the package registry, with notes explaining the problem, and consumers are informed through release notes and, if serious, an advisory. Internally, I enable protected tag rules so existing tags cannot be updated or deleted, and make releases go through an automated pipeline that tags only after verifying that the commit is on the release branch and passes checks. I then run a review to find how the mistake happened.`,
    f: ["Why not move the tag?","What does yanking a release do?"]
  },
  175: {
    a: `Obfuscated code in a build script is a red flag, because legitimate changes rarely need to hide what they do. I stop CI from running it with privileges: for pull requests from forks, secrets should not be exposed and workflows should require maintainer approval before running. I read the diff closely, with particular attention to scripts, workflow files, package install hooks and anything that downloads or evaluates remote content. To understand the obfuscated part, I decode it in an isolated sandbox with no credentials or network access to internal systems, never on my workstation. I ask the author to explain the reason and to replace it with readable code, and involve the security team if the explanation is unsatisfactory or the account looks compromised. Defensive controls include CODEOWNERS on pipeline files, pinning actions to commit hashes, least-privilege tokens, and scanning for suspicious patterns.`,
    f: ["Why are workflow files high risk?","Why do fork PRs not get secrets?"]
  },
  176: {
    a: `This is common and easy to fix if I have not pushed. The safest first step is to keep the commit reachable: either create a new branch at my current position, which captures it, or note the hash. If the right branch already exists, I switch to it and cherry-pick the commit. If the right branch does not exist yet, the new branch I just made is already it. Then I go back to the wrong branch and move it back to where it should be, with a hard reset to the previous commit or to its remote counterpart. Because the commit lives on the other branch, nothing is lost, and the reflog is also a backup. If I had already pushed the mistake to a shared branch, I would not rewrite it, and use revert instead and then re-apply the change on the correct branch through a normal pull request.`,
    f: ["What if you already pushed?","Why create the branch first?"]
  },
  177: {
    a: `A pull is a fetch followed by an integration step, and by default the integration is a merge. When my local branch has commits that the remote does not, and the remote also has new commits, Git creates a merge commit just to join them, which clutters history with commits like merge branch main into main. I explain the model and offer settings. Setting pull to rebase replays my local commits on top of the updated remote branch, giving linear history, which many teams prefer for feature branches. The fast-forward only setting refuses to create merge commits and tells the developer to decide consciously. I recommend the habit of fetching first and looking at what changed before integrating. I also explain that rebasing is for local, unpushed commits, and shared branches should not be rewritten, and enable autostash so uncommitted changes do not block the rebase.`,
    f: ["When is rebase on pull a bad idea?","What does ff-only do?"]
  }
};
